window.CYCLE2_PAGE = {"html": "<nav class=\"crumb\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><a href=\"#home\">Overview</a><span>/</span><span>Cycle 2</span></nav><header class=\"guide-heading\"><p class=\"kicker\">Cycle 2 | Guided explanation</p><h1>Cycle 2: Facilitate collaborative professional learning</h1><p>Cycle 2 asks you to help a small group of colleagues learn together, select one evidence-based strategy, put it into practice, and study what happens. Your task is not to give a polished presentation or obtain agreement with a plan you have already made. It is to make shared professional reasoning and action possible.</p><p>The ten parts preserve different kinds of evidence from that work. We will follow them in order, using the original templates, complete rubrics, and one connected fictional example.</p></header><nav class=\"c1-contents\" aria-label=\"Cycle 2 sections\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/limits\">Submission limits</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/foundation\">Understanding the work</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-map\">Rubric map</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/fictional-case\">Fictional example</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-a\">Part A</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-b\">Part B</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-c\">Part C</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-d\">Part D</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-e\">Part E</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-f\">Part F</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-g\">Part G</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-h\">Part H</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-i\">Part I</a><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-j\">Part J</a></nav><article class=\"reading guide-reading c1-reading\"><section id=\"limits\"><h2>What you submit, and the limits to plan around</h2><p>Read this before arranging meetings or collecting evidence. These are maxima, not targets. The original narrative template counts toward its page limit. N/A in the guide means no page or time maximum is specified for that part; it does not remove the evidence requirement.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"c1-limits\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Limit and files</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Context, educational focus, and community of practice</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 5 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Planning meeting agenda</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Planning meeting minutes</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Problem of practice and selected strategy</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 4 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">E</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Implementation meeting agendas</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">At least 3 meetings; all agendas in 1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">F</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Implementation meeting minutes</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">At least 3 meetings; all minutes in 1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">G</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Collaborative group work products</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 10 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">H</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Three implementation meeting video clips</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Exactly 3 video files; up to 5 minutes each</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">I</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Commentary on implementation meeting clips</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Reflective narrative</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 4 written pages OR 5 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Agendas and minutes are required even when their sample templates are optional. At least three implementation meetings occur over several weeks, after the separate planning meeting, with real implementation and initial evidence between meetings.</p><p>Part H is three clips of up to five minutes each. Part I's ten-minute alternative is commentary, not extra meeting footage. Part J's spoken alternative is five minutes, not Cycle 3's six. Written and verbal/ASL options are alternatives, not combined allowances.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=34\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Original submission specifications</a></p></section><section id=\"foundation\"><h2>Understand the work before filling in the templates</h2><h3>What makes this collaboration?</h3><p>Partners contribute evidence, question interpretations, influence the problem and strategy, implement agreed work, and help decide what findings mean. Your facilitation may include questions, summaries, time management, disagreement, and support between meetings. Explain how those actions affected the inquiry. Participation is not equitable merely because everyone was invited.</p><h3>Keep three decisions distinct</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Decision</th><th scope=\"col\">When and with whom</th><th scope=\"col\">Canyon View example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Educational focus</td><td data-label=\"When and with whom\">Initial investigation, with administrator or teacher-leader guidance</td><td data-label=\"Canyon View example\">Grade 9 evidence-based writing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Problem of practice</td><td data-label=\"When and with whom\">Planning meeting, jointly from relevant data</td><td data-label=\"Canyon View example\">Inconsistent instructional opportunities to model and practice explaining why evidence supports a claim.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">One evidence-based strategy</td><td data-label=\"When and with whom\">Planning meeting, jointly considering fit and research</td><td data-label=\"Canyon View example\">A Model-Practice-Reflect writing routine.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Then distinguish planning from implementation: the planning meeting decides what to try; the later meetings examine what was tried and what was learned. An agenda is an intention, minutes are a record, a work product is an artifact, a clip shows interaction, commentary analyzes it, and reflection examines your leadership across the cycle.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-map\"><h2>How the parts and rubrics fit together</h2><p>Each row names one rubric. Read its purpose now, then return to the complete five-level rubric beside the relevant work. A higher level includes the stated earlier levels; extra pages, polished language, or more charts alone do not establish it.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"c1-overview-map\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Rubric focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">1. Investigate</td><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Understand the setting before convening the group.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\"><strong>2.1: Professional learning context</strong></a><br>Analyze current professional learning and its relationship to student learning or well-being.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">1. Investigate</td><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Choose a justified focus and a purposeful group.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\"><strong>2.2: Focus and membership</strong></a><br>Connect the focus, data, school commitments, membership, and your relationships.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">2. Plan</td><td data-label=\"Part\">B-D</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Turn shared evidence into a changeable problem.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\"><strong>2.3: Collaborative problem definition</strong></a><br>Demonstrate the group's role in analyzing data and selecting a problem of practice.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">2. Plan</td><td data-label=\"Part\">B-D</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Agree on one strategy and a way to learn from it.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\"><strong>2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</strong></a><br>Explain collaborative selection and the strategy's potential effect on the identified need.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">E-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Make group reasoning visible in authentic meetings.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\"><strong>2.5: Facilitation of group learning</strong></a><br>Demonstrate and analyze facilitation, decisions, diverse perspectives, and focus.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">E-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Use initial findings to determine what happens next.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\"><strong>2.6: Results and next steps</strong></a><br>Show shared interpretation of results and collaborative next steps.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">4. Reflect</td><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Learn from the results and the group's feedback.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\"><strong>2.7: Reflection on facilitation</strong></a><br>Analyze equitable facilitation, strengths, growth, and professional next steps.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section id=\"fictional-case\"><h2>Fictional Example Case</h2><p class=\"source-note\">All school context, people, meetings, work samples, quotations, counts, and outcomes in this Canyon View case are invented for instruction. This is not Jordan High data, an assessed submission, or evidence that fieldwork occurred. Illustrative video moments are not recordings or scripts to stage.</p><p>Canyon View High is an invented setting. The candidate, a non-evaluative instructional coach, facilitates four partners as they examine reasoning in Grade 9 writing. Their matched instructional sample includes 24 students: 12 classified as English learners and 12 not classified as English learners. Those are the comparison groups in this example; neither group represents every learner at the school.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence point</th><th scope=\"col\">English learners</th><th scope=\"col\">Peers not classified as EL</th><th scope=\"col\">Total meeting criterion</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence point\">Baseline</td><td data-label=\"English learners\">4/12</td><td data-label=\"Peers not classified as EL\">8/12</td><td data-label=\"Total meeting criterion\">12/24</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence point\">Week 2</td><td data-label=\"English learners\">6/12</td><td data-label=\"Peers not classified as EL\">9/12</td><td data-label=\"Total meeting criterion\">15/24</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence point\">Week 4</td><td data-label=\"English learners\">8/12</td><td data-label=\"Peers not classified as EL\">10/12</td><td data-label=\"Total meeting criterion\">18/24</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence point\">Week 6</td><td data-label=\"English learners\">9/12</td><td data-label=\"Peers not classified as EL\">10/12</td><td data-label=\"Total meeting criterion\">19/24</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The criterion is whether a paragraph explains why its evidence supports the claim. The same sampled learners, comparable writing tasks, common criterion, and recorded scoring discussions support interpretation. This is a descriptive teaching example, not a causal study, a standardized assessment, or a Jordan High report. The later numbers appear here so you can follow the whole case; participants would not know them at the planning stage.</p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"A\"><h2 id=\"part-a\">Part A: Context, educational focus, and community of practice</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">1. Investigate | Up to 5 pages; 1 file</p><p>Begin by finding out how adults at the school currently learn together. A community of practice is not simply a committee that meets: its members examine a shared concern, learn through their work, try a response, and revisit evidence. Your leadership task is to make that shared inquiry possible.</p><p>The focus may grow from Cycle 1, but it does not have to. Secure guidance about a suitable educational focus, then form a group whose work can affect that focus. Form a group of 3 to 5 educators or educational partners in addition to yourself. At least 3 of them and you must be seen and heard in each implementation clip.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S1_A_ContextEdFocusCommunityPrac.docx\" download>Download the original Part A template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-A-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-A-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Inquiry into Collaborative Professional Learning</p><h3>1. Describe and analyze the role of collaborative professional learning at your school site/educational setting as it relates to student learning and/or well-being.</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. Provide information about any formal training offered to professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting within the past three years related to collaborative professional learning, communities of practice, or group learning process.</p><p>b. Describe and analyze the ways in which professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting currently engage with one another in professional learning. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of this professional learning in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>c. Describe and analyze the ways in which professional educators engage with one another in communities of practice. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of the community of practice approach in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>d. Describe how collaborative professional learning reflects evidence-based adult learning processes and cite relevant research to support your analysis.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address all four subparts. Establish what actually happened during the past three years, distinguish formal training from ongoing collaboration, and analyze evidence of impact or its absence. A meeting schedule proves an opportunity existed, not that adults or students learned.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At Canyon View, Year 1 training introduced common writing criteria, Year 2 sessions practiced scoring, and Year 3 added a monthly inquiry period. Department minutes show that most recent meetings still addressed deadlines. Two teams compared student drafts; neither routinely revisited the same learners after a change in instruction. Teachers report that shared scoring clarified expectations, but the available records do not establish improved student writing. The community model offers repeated examination of practice, while the one-time sessions chiefly introduced information. Research on professional development helps us ask whether teachers have opportunities to practice, receive feedback, and revisit evidence, not merely attend. <a href=\"https://learningpolicyinstitute.org/product/effective-teacher-professional-development-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Darling-Hammond, Hyler, and Gardner (2017)</a> led me to examine opportunities for rehearsal and feedback in our meeting records. I therefore reserved time for teachers to try a model and return with work. The review describes effective programs; it does not establish an effect of our short inquiry.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This distinguishes types of professional learning and the strength of evidence for their effects. Use your actual training records, minutes, work, and participant perspectives. <a href=\"https://learningpolicyinstitute.org/product/effective-teacher-professional-development-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Darling-Hammond, Hyler, and Gardner (2017)</a> identify features of effective professional development; their study does not evaluate this fictional school.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Inquiry into Collaborative Professional Learning</p><h3>2. As a result of your investigation and given the educational setting as well as available data related to student learning and/or well-being, describe the most important issues you will need to consider in preparing to facilitate and support a community of practice to engage in collaborative professional learning.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain what the investigation means for your facilitation. Name a small number of consequential issues and the response each requires.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Beyond the monthly schoolwide inquiry period, this volunteer group has 35 protected minutes every other week. Teachers have no common method for tracking the reasoning in student paragraphs. The ELD teacher reports that language explanations are sometimes mistaken for a lack of ideas. I will reserve protected work-analysis time, agree on a reasoning criterion, and invite language and disability expertise before the group selects a response.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A contextual detail becomes useful when it changes your plan. Do not replace analysis with a list of generic meeting norms.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>1. Describe the agreed-upon area of educational focus for improving student learning and/or well-being (obtained with guidance from one or more school administrators and/or teacher leaders) selected for the group\u2019s professional learning. Be sure to include a discussion of the relevant data that supports the selection of this focus and the connection between the educational focus and the vision, mission, and/or goals in your school site/educational setting.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Establish the broad educational focus with appropriate leadership guidance, evidence, and a verified school goal. Do not preselect the group's later problem or strategy here.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The principal and English teacher leader support examining Grade 9 evidence-based writing. In 24 matched baseline writing samples, 4 of 12 English learners and 8 of 12 students not classified as English learners explain how their evidence supports a claim. The fictional school improvement goal calls for equitable access to grade-level argument writing. These samples justify inquiry; they do not describe every ninth grader or establish a cause.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The focus is writing. The group must still determine the modifiable instructional problem and choose a strategy together.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>2. Provide a list of the job titles for each member of the community of practice group, detailing the specific reason(s) for each member\u2019s inclusion and referencing the area of educational focus.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Identify 3-5 partners in addition to yourself. Explain each person's contribution to this focus, not simply their availability.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The four partners are Chen and Rivera, Grade 9 English teachers who teach the sampled learners; Patel, an ELD teacher who can help interpret language demands and learner strengths; and Morgan, a special education co-teacher who can examine accessibility and accommodations. I facilitate as an instructional coach. Each partner contributes to both analysis and implementation within their assignment.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is five adults including the candidate, not five partners plus an unnamed facilitator. Record actual job titles and reasons without assuming expertise from demographic identity.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>3. Provide a description of each group member\u2019s demographics and how this reflects or is different from your school site/educational setting\u2019s demographics.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe each member's relevant demographics and compare the group with the school. Use information people appropriately provide; do not infer identity from names or appearance.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>In this invented context, Chen identifies as Asian American and speaks English and Mandarin; Rivera as Latina and speaks English and Spanish; Patel as South Asian American and speaks English and Gujarati; Morgan as White and English-speaking. The fictional student population includes substantial Latino, Black, and Asian communities and several home languages. No partner identifies as Black, and the group does not represent every family language. These differences make student perspectives and checks on our assumptions important; no member speaks for an entire group.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Comparison supports an honest account of representation and missing perspectives. Protect identities in actual assessment evidence and avoid turning demographics into explanations of competence.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>4. Provide a brief description of your relationship or positionality (e.g., coach, mentor, teacher colleague, administrator) to each member of the group.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain your relationship to each member and how power or trust may affect participation.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>I am a non-evaluative instructional coach to Chen and Rivera and a collaborating colleague of Patel and Morgan; I do not assign their evaluations. I previously planned a lesson with Chen, so her familiarity with my approach may make her more comfortable speaking first. I will use individual evidence review before discussion and invite interpretations from all four members before proposing a conclusion.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Positionality is your relationship and authority in this particular group. Claiming everyone is equal does not remove differences in status or familiarity.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q7\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>5. Explain how the work of this group is likely to advance conditions for students\u2019 learning and/or well-being in relation to the area of educational focus.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe a plausible pathway from adult learning to improved student conditions, while keeping the result prospective at this stage.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Shared analysis may help teachers distinguish a missing explanation from a language-access barrier. If the group learns to make reasoning visible and reviews who benefits, students may receive more consistent opportunities to explain and revise. We have not implemented a strategy yet; later work samples and meeting records will be needed to judge initial results.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A reasoned expectation is not an accomplished outcome. Name what you will need to observe before claiming progress.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-1\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Professional learning context</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how do these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being?</p><p>Analyze current professional learning and its relationship to student learning or well-being.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not describe the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. OR Candidate does not analyze how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing description or analysis can leave the purpose unexplained.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate minimally describes the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a vague analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Listing activities is not the same as interpreting their role or impact.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides a clear detailed description of the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a clear analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Describe and analyze the connection to student learning using the setting's evidence.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains the relationship between different forms of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how these practices have impacted student learning and/or well-being, providing evidence from observations, documentation, surveys, student work, and/or staff consultations.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Compare different forms and the evidence of their impacts.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate critiques collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting, referring to evidence-based adult learning processes and/or research that supports or refutes the practices and outlines implications for facilitating a community of practice at their school site/educational setting.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use adult-learning research to critique practice and explain implications.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 15 (printed page 11)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> Part A compares training, department routines, and inquiry work. The limited impact evidence is named rather than filled with an invented outcome.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-2\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Focus and membership</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate select an area of educational focus based on student data that corresponds to the vision, mission, and/or goals of their school site/educational setting? How does the candidate choose a group of educators/educational partners to participate in a community of practice in relation to the selected area of educational focus?</p><p>Connect the focus, data, school commitments, membership, and your relationships.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not select or selects an area of educational focus that is not based on student data or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate\u2019s school site/educational setting. OR Candidate provides little detail about demographic characteristics and/or does not provide reasons for member inclusion in the community of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing focus evidence or membership reasoning weakens the foundation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate selects and minimally describes an area of educational focus loosely based on student data and/or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate\u2019s school site/educational setting. Candidate lists group members and provides unclear or irrelevant details of their demographics and characteristics; rationale for member inclusion in the community of practice is not clear.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Minimal detail leaves the fit of the focus or partners unclear.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate selects an area of educational focus, clearly referring to data on student learning and/or well-being, and explains the connection to the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate\u2019s school site/educational setting. Candidate clearly describes community of practice group members by demographic characteristics, current job titles, assignments, and positional relationships and provides clear reasons for each member\u2019s inclusion in the group in relation to the selected area of educational focus.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain the focus and each member's relevant context, role, and relationship.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate articulates how the work of the group is likely to advance conditions for student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain how the group's work may improve student conditions.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides a rationale for including the specific members in their community of practice to ensure diverse viewpoints are represented and respected, leading to supports for all students to learn and to experience a positive sense of well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Show intentional attention to diverse perspectives and student learning or well-being.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 16 (printed page 12)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The case distinguishes an agreed writing focus from the problem the group later develops. Its demographic account identifies missing perspectives, not a claim of complete representation.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the three-year account compare professional-learning approaches and distinguish evidence of participation from evidence of impact?</li><li>Are the educational focus, school goal, 3-5 partners besides you, relevant perspectives, and your relationship with each partner explained?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-b\">Continue to Part B <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"B\"><h2 id=\"part-b\">Part B: Planning meeting agenda</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>The agenda is prepared before the planning meeting. It gives the group a route through evidence, problem definition, strategy selection, and implementation planning. The planning meeting is distinct from the later three implementation meetings.</p><p>An agenda is required; this particular agenda template is optional. A program-supported format may be used if it includes the required information. Allow real time for interpretation and disagreement. A full agenda is not evidence that every item was completed.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_B_PlanningMeetingAgenda_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Download the original Part B template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-B-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-B-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>1. Review the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan an opening that establishes the shared purpose and its connection to school goals.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Planning meeting, September 9, 3:15-4:00. All four partners attend, facilitated by the candidate (the instructional coach). Norms: examine evidence before explanation, invite each perspective, and record disagreement respectfully. First 5 minutes: connect Grade 9 argument writing to the school's equitable-access goal.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Include date, start/end time, participants and roles, focal group, goals, and norms in the header. An agenda records intended work, not decisions that have already occurred.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>2. Review and analyze data:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. Data source(s):</p><p>b. Discussion of data:</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Name the data and the questions the group will investigate; do not write the conclusion in advance.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 5-18: examine the 24 anonymized baseline paragraphs and the reasoning counts (English learners 4/12; peers not classified as EL 8/12). Compare two scoring disagreements and look at language supports documented in lesson materials. Ask: Where is reasoning visible? Which students demonstrate it orally but not yet in writing? What cannot this sample tell us?</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Both the source and planned discussion belong here. A group rate should lead back to actual work and opportunities to learn.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>3. Identify a problem of practice:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Create room for the group to define the problem from evidence.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 18-25: identify a current teaching practice that may be changed. Each partner proposes an interpretation, identifies supporting work, and names a competing explanation before the group drafts a problem of practice.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The agenda should invite a decision. It should not imply that your preferred problem was collaboratively selected before the meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>4. Addressing the problem of practice:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan a reasoned choice of one evidence-based strategy.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 25-34: compare the fit of a Model-Practice-Reflect writing routine with an approach limited to giving longer written comments. Consider evidence, instructional time, accessibility, and what the selected routine would look like in both English classrooms.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Use research to examine fit, not decorate a predetermined choice. <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/PracticeGuide/22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">WWC, Teaching Secondary Students to Write Effectively</a> supports explicitly teaching writing strategies through a Model-Practice-Reflect cycle.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>5. Implementation plan:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reserve time to decide who will implement, how adults will learn the strategy, and how evidence will be collected.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 34-40: plan a rehearsal using one common paragraph, two classroom uses per week for six weeks, a shared reasoning criterion, and a matched set of student samples at baseline and weeks 2, 4, and 6. Check accommodations and permitted storage.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> An implementation plan needs teachable actions and comparable evidence, not just a target date.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>6. Next steps and action items:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Close with responsibilities, support, and the next meeting.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 40-45: confirm owners and due dates, collect unresolved questions, and schedule implementation meetings for September 23, October 7, and October 21, each 3:15-3:50. Ask every partner to state what they will bring to the first meeting.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The next three meetings examine instruction that has actually taken place; they are not three further planning meetings.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the agenda identify date, time, roles, goals, norms, evidence, and genuine decision time?</li><li>Does it leave the problem and strategy open for shared analysis and reserve time for implementation responsibilities?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-c\">Continue to Part C <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"C\"><h2 id=\"part-c\">Part C: Planning meeting minutes</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Minutes preserve what happened: the evidence considered, interpretations and differing views, agreements, responsibilities, and next steps. They are neither a word-for-word transcript nor a polished account that erases uncertainty.</p><p>The minutes are required even though the supplied format is optional. Explain what changed from the agenda when that matters. If the group did not reach a decision, record the unfinished work and the follow-up instead of inventing consensus.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_C_PlanningMeetingMinutes_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Download the original Part C template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-C-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>1. Review the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Record what the group actually discussed about the school goal.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>September 9 minutes: Rivera connected the access goal to grade-level reasoning rather than easier assignments. Patel asked that language supports preserve the intellectual demand. The group agreed to examine access to explanations and revision opportunities.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Minutes preserve the reasoning and voices behind agreement. Do not simply copy the agenda into past tense.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>2. Review and analyze data:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Show the group's analysis and the limitations it noticed.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen initially counted three paragraphs with a quotation as successful. Morgan noted that the criterion requires explaining the quotation's relevance. After checking the common criterion together, the group retained the baseline counts of 4/12 English learners and 8/12 peers. Patel noted that two unsuccessful writers had offered relevant oral explanations.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The disagreement matters because it improved interpretation. Counts and examples should agree, and a sample must not be relabeled a schoolwide result.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>3. Identify a problem of practice:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Document the jointly selected, modifiable problem.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The group agreed: Grade 9 writing lessons do not yet provide consistent modeling and supported practice in connecting evidence to a claim, particularly when students are developing the language for that explanation. We will examine the instructional opportunity, not assume students lack motivation.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A problem of practice locates something adults can change. It is more specific than a broad performance gap.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>4. Addressing the problem of practice:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Record the selected strategy and why alternatives were not chosen.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Members selected a Model-Practice-Reflect routine: teachers think aloud while constructing an explanation, students practice with appropriate access supports, and students use the criterion to reflect on their writing. Rivera worried this could become copying; the group added comparison of two different explanations while retaining the routine's purpose. More teacher comments alone would not address the lack of visible modeling.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Consensus means a shared, workable understanding with concerns acknowledged. Silence or a majority vote does not by itself show that everyone understands the decision.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>5. Implementation plan:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Record concrete implementation and monitoring agreements.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen and Rivera will use the routine twice weekly. Patel will co-plan language supports and Morgan will check access to the written and oral directions. The candidate will arrange a 20-minute rehearsal by September 12. The group will use comparable prompts and the same criterion, retain the same 24 sampled learners where possible, and record absences or missing work separately.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The minutes now establish what was decided. The agenda only shows that the topic was planned.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>6. Next steps and action items:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Capture owners, deadlines, support, and follow-through.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen drafts the first model by September 11; Rivera brings a contrasting model; Patel reviews supports by September 12; Morgan checks accessibility the same day. The candidate prepares the tracking sheet and rehearsal. Everyone brings anonymized initial work and a brief implementation log on September 23. Members confirm that no additional grading category is being imposed.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This makes the commitments auditable. Later minutes should show whether the tasks occurred and how unmet commitments were handled.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Do the minutes preserve differing interpretations, the shared problem and strategy, and reasons for the choice?</li><li>Are owners, support, due dates, unresolved questions, and follow-through recorded as decisions actually made?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-d\">Continue to Part D <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"D\"><h2 id=\"part-d\">Part D: Problem of practice and selected strategy</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | Up to 4 pages; 1 file</p><p>Now explain the reasoning behind the planning meeting. The agenda and minutes document the event; Part D analyzes how the group used evidence and how your facilitation supported the decisions. Keep the broad educational focus, specific problem of practice, and selected strategy distinct.</p><p>The supplied Word template labels its two sections II and III; the guide's directions refer to the corresponding sections as I and II. The original template labels and prompts are retained here. Match by section title and task, and do not renumber the official template yourself.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_D_ProbPracDescStrategy.docx\" download>Download the original Part D template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-D-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-D-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Collaboratively Select a Problem of Practice Based on Student Data and Data from Your Professional Context</p><h3>1. Describe how you: (a) worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the educational focus; (b) selected a problem of practice based on the area of educational focus; and (c) addressed how practitioners could potentially improve student learning and/or well-being by improving the current practices at your school site/educational setting.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how your facilitation connected group analysis, problem selection, and a change in practice. Refer to evidence of the process rather than announcing the final problem alone.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>I asked members to score independently before discussion. When our judgments differed, we returned to the explanation criterion and the work. This shifted discussion from students' effort to the opportunities teachers provide to model and practice reasoning. The minutes record Patel's oral-language counterexample and the resulting problem statement.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Explain your action, the group's response, and why that response matters. A list of meeting activities leaves the leadership reasoning invisible.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Collaboratively Select a Problem of Practice Based on Student Data and Data from Your Professional Context</p><h3>2. Explain how you worked collaboratively with the group to review your collected data on student demographics, student learning and academic performance or well-being, and other relevant data differentiated by student groups.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how different student groups and relevant contextual evidence were examined together.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>We compared 4/12 and 8/12 baseline results, then examined examples within both groups. Two English learners explained their reasoning orally; four peers also lacked written reasoning. We reviewed lesson materials and noted differences in explicit modeling. We did not conclude that English-learner status caused the pattern or that all English learners needed the same support.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Disaggregation reveals differences but does not explain their causes. Work samples and context help test interpretations without stereotyping.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Collaboratively Select a Problem of Practice Based on Student Data and Data from Your Professional Context</p><h3>3. Describe the ways you facilitated the meeting to acknowledge how the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current practices in your professional context.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Show how you helped the group identify a practice it could realistically change.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>When a member proposed that students should try harder, I asked what students had been shown and what instructional opportunity we controlled. Members identified inconsistent modeling and supported practice. We narrowed the inquiry to a repeatable writing routine within existing lessons, not a new schoolwide program.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The redirect concerns evidence and professional responsibility. Do not invent a conversation to make your actual facilitation sound stronger.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Collaboratively Select a Problem of Practice Based on Student Data and Data from Your Professional Context</p><h3>4. How does the identified problem of practice: (a) correspond to the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/educational setting; (b) reflect California state standards and/or curriculum frameworks or equivalent, or other applicable standards; and (c) address existing differences in student group performance or well-being?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Connect the problem to the school commitment, an applicable content standard, and the observed group differences.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The school goal calls for equitable access to grade-level writing. The problem concerns connecting evidence with a claim, one component of <a href=\"https://www2.cde.ca.gov/cacs/id/web/5925\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">California W.9-10.1</a>, which addresses argument writing with reasoned support and appropriate evidence. A single paragraph does not demonstrate the whole standard. The baseline difference is four students in equally sized sampled groups, while unsuccessful work appears in both groups. Our response must maintain the goal and vary access supports.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Verify and cite your actual grade/content standard. A CSTP focus for teaching practice does not replace the student content standard requested here.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">III. Identify a Strategy to Address the Problem of Practice</p><h3>1. Explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting in selecting one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice, potentially leading to improved student learning and/or well-being across student groups. Be sure to include how you facilitated the group to help make their selection.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how the group chose one strategy together, including your facilitation and the rationale for its fit.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>I asked each member to compare a Model-Practice-Reflect routine with longer written comments using the same criteria: the observed need, research, feasibility, and accessibility. Patel and Morgan identified supports needed to make practice usable. Members selected the routine because it makes the reasoning process available before students work independently; they retained Rivera's concern about copying as a monitoring question. We used Recommendation 1 in <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/PracticeGuide/22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">WWC, Teaching Secondary Students to Write Effectively</a> to specify modeling, practice, and reflection. This changed our choice from adding comments after writing to making the reasoning process visible during instruction. The local language supports remain adaptations to monitor.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A research citation cannot establish that a decision was collaborative. Explain both the choice and the process that produced it.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">III. Identify a Strategy to Address the Problem of Practice</p><h3>2. Describe the group\u2019s rationale for selecting this evidence-based strategy. How will the group learn this strategy and how to implement it? What challenges do you anticipate the group might encounter and how could they be addressed?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe adult learning, likely challenges, and specific supports for implementation.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Teachers will rehearse the think-aloud, compare models, and examine student practice together before using the routine. A challenge is that fluent writing may conceal missing reasoning; the common criterion separates the explanation from surface correctness. Time is another constraint, so the model replaces part of an existing lesson rather than adding a separate intervention period.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The research supports the instructional approach, not every local adaptation. Document what is retained, what is adapted, and why the adaptation should still address the problem.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q7\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">III. Identify a Strategy to Address the Problem of Practice</p><h3>3. What improvements for student learning and/or well-being related to the problem of practice do the group expect the selected evidence-based strategy will yield?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>State the expected improvement as an expectation that can be examined.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>We expect more sampled students to explain how evidence supports their claim, with attention to progress among English learners and students who initially struggle in either group. We also expect teachers to make the criterion more explicit. We will not equate participation, completed worksheets, or more meetings with improved reasoning.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Keep the outcome connected to the learning problem. Establish how it will be recognized before reviewing results.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q8\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">III. Identify a Strategy to Address the Problem of Practice</p><h3>4. How will you facilitate the group in determining a monitoring process to understand the impact of the evidence-based strategy? Describe how you and the group plan to monitor and analyze initial implementation.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe a monitoring process the group helped design.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At weeks 2, 4, and 6, the group will score comparable paragraphs using the baseline criterion and review disagreements with the actual work. We will preserve student sample IDs, note missing work, separate the two group counts, and review implementation logs. Each meeting will consider successful cases, persistent difficulties, and whether an adaptation is needed.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Comparable does not mean identical. Record task differences and absences, and avoid treating a small descriptive inquiry as a causal evaluation.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-3\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: Using the agreed-upon area of educational focus as a starting point, how does the candidate work collaboratively with the group to review data sets and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being? How does the candidate work with the group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current instructional practice at their school site/educational setting?</p><p>Demonstrate the group's role in analyzing data and selecting a problem of practice.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not involve the group in analyzing data sets to select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. OR Candidate provides no evidence that they worked collaboratively with the group to select the problem of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A problem selected without the group or evidence is not collaborative inquiry.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate minimally involves the group in the analysis of data sets and/or the selection of the problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate minimally involves the group in the selection of the problem of practice and/or minimally describes how the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Participation or data use may be too limited to establish shared reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly explains how they worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the selected educational focus and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate clearly describes how they worked collaboratively with group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain shared analysis, the selected problem, and the practice that can change.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate differentiates the academic performance for more than one student group and/or issue of well-being, providing supporting student data/evidence and explains how they facilitated the group to collaboratively address these differences in defining the problem of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Differentiate needs across more than one student group with evidence.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains in detail both how they facilitated the collaborative process with the group members to analyze student data/evidence and why specific facilitation skills were used.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Analyze how and why your facilitation advanced the group's reasoning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5B, 5C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 20 (printed page 16)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The baseline counts, oral-language counterexample, and scoring disagreement show how the inquiry moved toward instructional opportunity.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-4\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy to address the selected problem of practice? How does the candidate describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being?</p><p>Explain collaborative selection and the strategy's potential effect on the identified need.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate does not describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A strategy without joint selection or a relevant rationale is insufficient.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate vaguely explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate minimally discusses the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A vague choice leaves the expected benefit unexplained.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate clearly describes the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain the process, evidence base, and potential effect.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes in detail how the collaboratively determined evidence-based strategy will have a positive impact on student learning and/or well-being across specific student groups.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Specify how different student groups may benefit.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate thoroughly explains potential implementation implications and/or challenges across multiple student groups and suggests feasible next steps for each group, as appropriate.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Anticipate implementation challenges across groups and feasible responses.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 21 (printed page 17)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The routine has a research basis, but local adaptations and actual benefits still require examination. Research is not a guarantee for Canyon View.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Can the reader trace the problem to the group analysis and the strategy to research and the identified need?</li><li>Have you explained your facilitation, likely implementation challenges, expected benefits across groups, and collaborative monitoring within four pages?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-e\">Continue to Part E <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"E\"><h2 id=\"part-e\">Part E: Implementation meeting agendas</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | At least 3 meetings; all agendas in 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Implementation means the strategy is actually used in practice over several weeks. Each implementation meeting examines new evidence from that use. Three meetings about what teachers might do later are not a substitute.</p><p>Plan at least three implementation meetings and video-record each one. Keep dates, start/end times, grade level, participants and roles, norms, goals, the named strategy, new evidence, a summary process, and next steps visible. These optional agenda layouts repeat because each meeting needs its own plan.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_E_AgendasImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Download the original Part E template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 1 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 1 of 3. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-E-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 1 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 1 of 3. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 2 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 2 of 3. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-E-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 2 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 2 of 3. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 3 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 3 of 3. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-E-3.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 3 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 3 of 3. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 1</p><h3>1. Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan what the group will examine after instruction, including successful and difficult cases.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Meeting 1, September 23, 3:15-3:50, Grade 9; same four partners and the candidate as instructional coach. Norms remain evidence first and all voices before a conclusion. Bring the first two weeks of work and the September 9 planning commitments. Examine whether the rehearsed models made reasoning visible, and whether students are explaining rather than copying.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The agenda carries forward a known decision and an open question. New findings belong in the minutes after this meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 1</p><h3>2. Summary of the meeting:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reserve time for members to check the accuracy of the shared summary.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minute 25 of Meeting 1, ask members to state the strongest supported conclusion, a limitation, and any disagreement that should remain in the record.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is a planned summary process. The agreed conclusion belongs in the minutes after the meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 1</p><h3>3. Next steps for the PLC:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan a close that establishes action, support, and the next meeting.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minutes 30-35 of Meeting 1, confirm who will do what, the due date, any support required, and the next meeting date. Ask whether the proposed action responds to the reviewed learning evidence.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A recurring heading can remain the same while the evidence and decisions change. Reusing identical completed agendas without new data obscures implementation.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 2</p><h3>1. Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan what the group will examine after instruction, including successful and difficult cases.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Meeting 2, October 7, 3:15-3:50, Grade 9; same four partners and the candidate as instructional coach. Norms remain evidence first and all voices before a conclusion. Revisit the September 23 decision to compare contrasting explanations. Bring the models completed by September 25 and the new matched paragraphs. Ask whether the contrast clarified reasoning and which learners still need another way to rehearse their ideas.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The agenda carries forward a known decision and an open question. New findings belong in the minutes after this meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 2</p><h3>2. Summary of the meeting:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reserve time for members to check the accuracy of the shared summary.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minute 25 of Meeting 2, ask members what the new work suggests about the contrasting models, which learners still need support, and what disagreements remain about the sentence frame.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is a planned summary process. The agreed conclusion belongs in the minutes after the meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 2</p><h3>3. Next steps for the PLC:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan a close that establishes action, support, and the next meeting.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minutes 30-35 of Meeting 2, agree on the next adjustment in light of the evidence just reviewed. Consider whether oral rehearsal is worth trying while keeping the writing criterion unchanged; assign responsibilities and identify work to bring on October 21.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A recurring heading can remain the same while the evidence and decisions change. Reusing identical completed agendas without new data obscures implementation.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q7\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 3</p><h3>1. Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan what the group will examine after instruction, including successful and difficult cases.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Meeting 3, October 21, 3:15-3:50, Grade 9; same four partners and the candidate as instructional coach. Norms remain evidence first and all voices before a conclusion. Revisit the October 7 oral-rehearsal adjustment. Bring initial and revised pairs and notes on who used the support. Ask whether the written explanation meets the unchanged criterion, including cases that remain difficult.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The agenda carries forward a known decision and an open question. New findings belong in the minutes after this meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q8\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 3</p><h3>2. Summary of the meeting:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reserve time for members to check the accuracy of the shared summary.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minute 25 of Meeting 3, ask members what the week-6 work supports, which learning needs remain, and what this small sample cannot show about schoolwide effectiveness.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is a planned summary process. The agreed conclusion belongs in the minutes after the meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q9\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 3</p><h3>3. Next steps for the PLC:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan a close that establishes action, support, and the next meeting.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minutes 30-35 of Meeting 3, decide whether further inquiry or a wider trial is warranted. Agree on the evidence needed, responsibilities, support, and a follow-up date before closing.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A recurring heading can remain the same while the evidence and decisions change. Reusing identical completed agendas without new data obscures implementation.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are there agendas for at least three implementation meetings after the planning meeting, combined in one file?</li><li>Does each agenda carry forward the previous decision and identify new work and unresolved questions without predicting findings?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-f\">Continue to Part F <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"F\"><h2 id=\"part-f\">Part F: Implementation meeting minutes</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | At least 3 meetings; all minutes in 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Use the minutes to connect one meeting to the next. What was tried after the last decision? What evidence returned? What did members conclude or question? What support did you agree to provide?</p><p>The original format asks what is going well, what needs adjustment, participant reflections, a summary, and next steps. Document actual agreed support to individuals and the group. The fictional records below illustrate those fields; they do not replace your own meeting records.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_F_MinutesImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Download the original Part F template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-F-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-F-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><h3>Original meeting-record fields</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>Implementation Meeting 1: Minutes</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a. What is going well?</p><p>b. What needs adjustment?</p><p>c. PLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p><p>Implementation Meeting 2: Minutes</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a. What is going well?</p><p>b. What needs adjustment?</p><p>c. PLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p><p>Implementation Meeting 3: Minutes</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a. What is going well?</p><p>b. What needs adjustment?</p><p>c. PLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><h3>Three connected fictional meeting records</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Meeting and findings</th><th scope=\"col\">Discussion and decision</th><th scope=\"col\">Support and follow-through</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Meeting and findings\"><strong>1: September 23, 3:15-3:50</strong><br>15/24; EL 6/12, peers 9/12. Students identify a claim more consistently, but three sampled explanations merely repeat evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Discussion and decision\">Patel notices that a frame helps students begin; Rivera worries it masks missing reasoning. Compare two different explanations in the next model; keep the reasoning criterion unchanged.</td><td data-label=\"Support and follow-through\">Chen and Rivera prepare models by September 25; Patel checks language access and Morgan checks accessibility; coach hosts a 15-minute rehearsal.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Meeting and findings\"><strong>2: October 7, 3:15-3:50</strong><br>18/24; EL 8/12, peers 10/12. Several revised paragraphs now explain relevance; six still do not meet the criterion.</td><td data-label=\"Discussion and decision\">Morgan reports that an oral rehearsal helped one learner; Chen asks how to distinguish the support from lowering the target. Retain the routine and permit appropriate oral rehearsal before written revision; examine the resulting writing against the same criterion.</td><td data-label=\"Support and follow-through\">Teachers retain initial and revised work; Patel and Morgan co-plan supports by October 9; coach assembles matched evidence for October 21.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Meeting and findings\"><strong>3: October 21, 3:15-3:50</strong><br>19/24; EL 9/12, peers 10/12. Progress is uneven and five students still need support; the later gain is smaller.</td><td data-label=\"Discussion and decision\">Rivera warns that newer topics may affect results; all members request individual-case review before expansion. Continue a short inquiry with those learning needs; do not announce schoolwide effectiveness from this sample.</td><td data-label=\"Support and follow-through\">Teachers bring two contrasting cases in two weeks; specialists plan supports; coach arranges time and invites written feedback on the inquiry.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>All five adults attended these fictional meetings. The October 7 minutes record completion of the contrasting models agreed on September 23; the October 21 minutes record review of the initial and revised work requested October 7. If your actual attendance or follow-through differs, record the difference. Do not fill a gap with a statement that the template seems to expect.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Do the minutes cover all three implementation meetings and distinguish findings, interpretations, decisions, and member reflections?</li><li>Do later records show whether earlier commitments occurred, with owners, dates, and support when plans changed?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-g\">Continue to Part G <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"G\"><h2 id=\"part-g\">Part G: Collaborative group work products</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | Up to 10 pages; 1 file</p><p>Submit selected products of the community's collaborative work, not only a narrative saying the group made something. A common scoring tool, joint analysis, annotated instructional model, or monitoring document may make the learning process visible when it genuinely came from this group.</p><p>Choose products that explain decisions and progress. Label each with its purpose and when the group used it. Protect identities. A stack of unrelated worksheets is less informative than a small, coherent set that connects the problem, strategy, and results.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/guide-part-G.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><h3>What the products themselves can show</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Fictional product</th><th scope=\"col\">Excerpt from the product</th><th scope=\"col\">Why the group retained it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Fictional product\">G1: Common reasoning criterion</td><td data-label=\"Excerpt from the product\">Meets: explains why a relevant piece of evidence supports the claim. Not yet: quotes or restates evidence without explaining its connection. Surface correctness is examined separately.</td><td data-label=\"Why the group retained it\">Agreed at planning; reused at all three implementation meetings.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Fictional product\">G2: Joint model annotation</td><td data-label=\"Excerpt from the product\">Model A: \"The character returns the wallet. This shows responsibility because the action protects another person's belongings even when no one is watching.\" Annotation: the because clause explains why the evidence supports the claim. Model B only repeats that the wallet was returned.</td><td data-label=\"Why the group retained it\">Members contrast reasoning with repetition; the language support should not replace the intellectual work.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Fictional product\">G3: Matched monitoring record</td><td data-label=\"Excerpt from the product\">Baseline: EL 4/12, peers 8/12, total 12/24. Week 2: 6/12, 9/12, 15/24. Week 4: 8/12, 10/12, 18/24. Week 6: 9/12, 10/12, 19/24. No missing sampled work in this invented dataset.</td><td data-label=\"Why the group retained it\">Comparable prompts and one criterion support descriptive comparison. Topic, instruction, and time remain possible influences.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Fictional product\">G4: Joint adjustment record</td><td data-label=\"Excerpt from the product\">October 7: retain modeling, practice, and reflection; allow appropriate oral rehearsal, then judge the written explanation using the same criterion. Review initial/revised pairs October 21.</td><td data-label=\"Why the group retained it\">Connects feedback, a decision, implementation, and a later review.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These text excerpts are fictional examples of artifacts, not evidence that a real group produced them. In your own Part G, include the actual selected products within the ten-page total.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are these actual products of this group, labeled by purpose and use, rather than a narrative describing absent artifacts?</li><li>Does the single combined file fit ten pages and connect the products to the meetings, clips, and commentary?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-h\">Continue to Part H <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"H\"><h2 id=\"part-h\">Part H: Three implementation meeting video clips</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | Exactly 3 video files; up to 5 minutes each</p><p>Record all implementation meetings so authentic facilitation and changes over time can be examined. Plan consent, camera placement, and audio beforehand. The candidate and at least three partners must be seen and heard as directed. The planning meeting is a separate event and is not the source of these required implementation clips.</p><p>Select three clips addressing the official functions below. The guide does not require one clip from each meeting, but all three implementation meetings and their agendas/minutes remain required. Follow the guide's editing directions and use only the minimum editing needed to meet the specified constructs and limits; do not stage or script the assessed interaction.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/guide-part-H.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Official clip function</th><th scope=\"col\">Illustrative selection</th><th scope=\"col\">What must be visible</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Official clip function\">Clip 1: Setting the Purpose and Data Review</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative selection\">4:30 from implementation meeting 1</td><td data-label=\"What must be visible\">Purpose and problem of practice; members examining implementation evidence, not the facilitator reading a report alone.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Official clip function\">Clip 2: Professional Collaboration</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative selection\">4:40 from implementation meeting 2</td><td data-label=\"What must be visible\">Contributions, differing perspectives, feedback, focus, and your facilitation of shared reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Official clip function\">Clip 3: Results and Next Steps</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative selection\">4:30 from implementation meeting 3</td><td data-label=\"What must be visible\">Joint interpretation of initial results and agreement about next steps.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The example durations all fit the five-minute individual maximum. They are planning illustrations, not actual media files. A total below 15 minutes does not excuse one clip exceeding 5 minutes. Keep original footage in authorized storage and inspect the uploaded files, not just the editing project.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are there exactly three authentic implementation clips, each no longer than five minutes, addressing the three named functions?</li><li>Can the candidate and at least three partners be seen and heard as required, with consent and usable sound, and are all implementation meetings recorded?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-i\">Continue to Part I <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"I\"><h2 id=\"part-i\">Part I: Commentary on implementation meeting clips</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>The video shows an event. Commentary explains the leadership reasoning the event alone may not reveal. For each relevant moment, identify the submitted clip number and a precise timestamp, explain what you did, why, and the impact supported by the group's response.</p><p>Address all five prompts at least once across the three clips. Do not give the entire five-minute clip as the timestamp for every claim. Commentary that describes an event absent from the submitted clips does not become evidence because the prose is persuasive.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_I_CommentaryImplementationMeetings.docx\" download>Download the original Part I template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-I-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-I-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>1. Describe how you set the purpose for the meetings/reviewed the problem of practice with the group.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Connect a specific video moment to the meeting purpose. Explain why the action helped the group work, not only what you announced.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 1, 00:15-00:55: I returned to the problem of inconsistent opportunities to practice reasoning and asked members to identify the work that would let us examine progress. Chen brought the matched sample table into the discussion. This kept the group focused on learning rather than the number of lessons completed.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is a model of commentary structure, not a real clip. In your submission the moment, response, and timestamp must be visible or audible in the actual submitted file.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>2. Describe how you and the community of practice evaluated data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how you and the group interpreted initial implementation evidence.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 1, 01:10-03:20: members compare baseline 12/24 with week-2 15/24, then examine one paragraph without reasoning. I ask which improvement is visible and what remains uncertain. Patel identifies that a sentence frame helped participation but did not always produce an explanation. The group distinguishes increased completion from the target reasoning.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> An improved total is not sufficient analysis. Explain which evidence supported the conclusion and which evidence challenged it.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>3. Describe any agreements or decisions made by the group and how you facilitated the community of practice to arrive at a consensual understanding of any initial implementation results.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain the shared understanding reached and how you helped members reach it.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 2, 00:45-02:10: Rivera questions whether the frame encourages copying. I invite a comparison of two student responses before summarizing a possible agreement. Members agree to retain the routine, compare contrasting models, and let students explain orally before revising when appropriate. Each member states the implication for their role. This is October 7, meeting 2: Rivera is revisiting the copying concern and contrasting-model adjustment discussed September 23, not first proposing it. The new decision is appropriate oral rehearsal, recorded in the meeting-2 minutes and product G4 for review October 21.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Agreement should be grounded in the recorded discussion. A facilitator's summary alone does not prove consensual understanding.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>4. Describe how you</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>valued and respected diverse viewpoints,</p><p>responded to individual and collective feedback,</p><p>maintained the group\u2019s focus and energy, and</p><p>monitored progress toward the goals of the meetings.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address every bullet: differing views, feedback, energy and focus, and progress. Tie each claim to an observable action.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 2, 02:15-04:20: I invite Morgan's access concern before finalizing the decision, acknowledge Rivera's criticism of the model, and ask the group to compare both with the work. I return to the reasoning criterion when discussion turns to handwriting, then check the agreed action against the meeting goal. Patel adds a support that the initial proposal omitted.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Respect is demonstrated by what happens to a contribution, not merely by saying everyone was valued. Select actual evidence; do not claim all four bullets from a moment that only shows one.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>5. Describe how you collaborated with the group to determine any next steps for implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Show a joint decision about what happens after the meeting, including support and responsibility.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 3, 02:00-04:10: after reviewing week-6 results of 19/24, members identify five learners still needing support. Chen proposes a further short cycle and Rivera asks for time to compare individual work. We agree that teachers will bring two contrasting samples, Patel and Morgan will co-plan appropriate supports, and I will schedule a follow-up in two weeks.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A next step belongs to the group, with named responsibilities and a reason from the findings. It is not simply your recommendation to continue.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-5\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze a variety of their facilitation skills of group learning\u2014 including documenting group decisions, supporting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy\u2014 while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, feedback, and progress during the implementation meetings?</p><p>Demonstrate and analyze facilitation, decisions, diverse perspectives, and focus.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not demonstrate facilitation skills during the implementation meetings. OR Candidate\u2019s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group learning or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A summary of a meeting is not an analysis of demonstrated facilitation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate demonstrates minimal facilitation skills that are vaguely supportive of group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate\u2019s commentary does not clearly provide an analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Minimal evidence or vague commentary leaves actions and effects unclear.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly demonstrates facilitation skills that support group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, including documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate\u2019s commentary provides a clear analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Show the actions and explain their purpose and visible impact.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate purposefully seeks diverse viewpoints and clearly encourages all members of the community to speak and share ideas during meetings.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Intentionally invite contributions from every member.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses their commentary to critique their facilitation skills used during the implementation meetings based on group learning processes and/or research that supports facilitation practices.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use relevant research to critique your own facilitation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) \u2022 Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) \u2022 Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 27 (printed page 23)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The illustrative moments show what commentary can analyze. They do not constitute the candidate's real video evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-6\"><h3>Rubric 2.6: Results and next steps</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate facilitate a discussion that leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results? How does the candidate collaboratively lead the group in jointly determining next steps?</p><p>Show shared interpretation of results and collaborative next steps.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not facilitate a discussion about implementation results. OR Next steps are not determined. OR Candidate\u2019s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group discussion or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing results, joint next steps, or analysis leaves the inquiry incomplete.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate facilitates a discussion that does not clearly lead to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. It is not clear that the candidate works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine the next steps. Candidate\u2019s commentary provides a vague analysis of their facilitation of group discussions.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Unclear consensus or one-sided decisions weaken the account.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate facilitates a discussion that clearly leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. Candidate clearly works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine next steps. Candidate\u2019s commentary provides a clear analysis of their facilitation of the group discussions.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Demonstrate shared understanding and analyze the process.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate consistently addresses initial results and next steps to inform the collaborative implementation process with group members both within and between meetings.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Show follow-through within and between meetings.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate collaborates with the group to adjust the implementation strategy as needed in response to its challenges or successes.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Show joint adaptation in response to successes and challenges.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) \u2022 Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) \u2022 Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1B CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 28 (printed page 24)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> Three rounds of matched work and minutes connect decisions over time. The group does not call a small descriptive pattern proof that the routine caused improvement.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are all five prompts addressed across the three clips, with precise timestamps checked against the submitted files?</li><li>Does commentary explain your action, purpose, and supported effect, within eight written pages OR ten minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-j\">Continue to Part J <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"J\"><h2 id=\"part-j\">Part J: Reflective narrative</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">4. Reflect | Up to 4 written pages OR 5 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>Reflection asks what the process and initial results reveal about your leadership. Use participant feedback and the evidence from this cycle to examine your contribution, not to declare that the group was successful because meetings occurred.</p><p>You can acknowledge mixed results and still analyze strong professional learning. Equitable facilitation includes whose knowledge shaped decisions, how disagreement was handled, and whether your support made meaningful participation possible.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Download the original Part J template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-J-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>1. Refer to specific evidence (including agendas, minutes, group work products, implementation data, videos, and/or participant feedback) to analyze how your leadership actions influenced the community of practice\u2019s progress toward its goal. Reflect on initial implementation results and feedback from the group to evaluate your strengths and areas for growth as a facilitator. Make explicit connections between the evidence, your leadership skills, equitable facilitation practices, and your next steps for professional growth.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Use results and group feedback to analyze your facilitation, linking evidence to a strength, a growth need, and a next step.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Matched samples improved from 12/24 to 19/24 meeting the reasoning criterion; English learners moved from 4/12 to 9/12 and peers from 8/12 to 10/12. This supports a description of initial progress, not proof of causation. The October 7 (Meeting 2, week 4) minutes show that inviting disagreement led to a useful adjustment. Members valued the work comparison but said my first agenda allowed too little time for interpretation. I need to plan discussion around the evidence, not fill every minute with tasks.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Results concern students; reflection concerns your leadership contribution and limitations. Both matter, and one cannot substitute for the other.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>2. Refer to specific evidence (including the agendas and minutes, group work products and initial implementation results, videos and commentary, and/or group feedback on the process) of how effectively you maintained a high standard of professionalism, integrity, and equity (e.g., respect for diverse viewpoints) as you worked with members of the community of practice.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain professionalism, integrity, and equity through specific choices across the process.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>I retained the unsuccessful samples alongside improving work, protected identities, and did not report a missing sample as a failure. The planning minutes and Clip 2 show that Patel's and Morgan's concerns changed the plan. I asked members to critique the routine even when I had advocated it. However, Chen spoke first in two meetings; future facilitation will vary the order and invite written interpretations before discussion.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> An honest limitation can support analysis. General claims of fairness are less useful than evidence of whose ideas influenced decisions and where participation still needs improvement.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>3. What aspects of school culture and context influenced the facilitation of a community of practice at your school site/educational setting? What actions did you take to respond to these contextually based challenges or supports?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain the effect of culture and context, including your response.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The protected biweekly period made sustained inquiry possible, but a history of deadline-driven meetings made some members expect announcements rather than joint work. I sent anonymized samples beforehand and used the first five minutes to revisit the inquiry purpose. When an assembly interrupted one lesson, the group recorded the implementation difference instead of pretending all classrooms received the same opportunity.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Context is not background decoration. Show how it changed your choices and what effect that response had.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>4. Based on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 2, identify specific professional learning goals and describe your next steps for growth.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Set a specific professional learning goal from your analysis, with a way to examine growth.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>During the next six-week inquiry, I will collect individual interpretations before group discussion, track whose contributions change decisions, and ask members whether they had meaningful influence. I will review two recordings with my mentor and compare the decision records. Research on active, collaborative professional learning will help me examine whether my facilitation supports adult reasoning rather than only orderly participation.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A goal such as be a better leader is too broad. Name the practice, timeframe, evidence, and professional learning resource; do not claim a research-informed change has already been tested.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-7\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on facilitation</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate reflect upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills and practices to make specific connections to their strengths and areas for growth, and identify next steps, referring to specific evidence from any of the four steps? How does the candidate demonstrate a variety of leadership skills used to equitably facilitate a community of practice?</p><p>Analyze equitable facilitation, strengths, growth, and professional next steps.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides no connection to initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make specific connections to their strengths or areas for growth, or next steps related to leadership practices. OR Candidate does not include specific evidence from any of the cycle\u2019s four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. OR Candidate does not describe their leadership practice using an equitable leadership approach to facilitate a community of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Unsupported reflection, missing results or feedback, or no equity connection creates gaps.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate vaguely or inaccurately reflects upon initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and/or next steps. Candidate vaguely refers to evidence from any of the cycle\u2019s four steps to support their reflections and/or inform their analysis. Candidate vaguely demonstrates their capacity to facilitate a community of practice. It is not clear that the candidate has an equitable approach to leadership practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Vague references do not show how evidence supports the judgment.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly reflects upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills, making specific connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and next steps. Candidate clearly refers to specific, appropriate evidence from any of the four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. Candidate clearly demonstrates how their leadership practice equitably facilitated a community of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Make explicit evidence-to-leadership-to-growth connections.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides a deep analysis that illustrates how they maintained professionalism and integrity as well as employed an equitable leadership approach consistently throughout the community of practice inquiry process.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity across the inquiry.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate consults evidence-based practices and/or research related to equitable leadership development, adult learning, and/or group facilitation, then uses the information to analyze their own leadership practices and set professional goals during this cycle.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use relevant research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 5 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 31 (printed page 27)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The reflection uses member criticism as evidence for change. A self-selected example cannot establish an assessor score.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does reflection use initial results and partner feedback to explain equitable facilitation, strengths, and growth?</li><li>Is the next professional-learning goal specific and supported by the analysis, within four pages OR five minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section></section><section id=\"final-check\"><h2>Review the cycle as one connected set of evidence</h2><p>Trace one line through Parts A-J: the focus leads to a collaboratively defined problem; the strategy responds to that problem; implementation records show actual use; products and clips make shared learning visible; commentary explains your actions; reflection uses results and feedback to identify growth.</p><p>Check dates, participant roles, sample sizes, the strategy name, clip labels, and next-step commitments across files. Make sure the planning meeting has not been counted as an implementation meeting. Verify all three implementation meetings, exactly three submitted clips, and the separate commentary and reflection limits.</p><p>The worked responses are longer teaching explanations distributed across this page, not a page-fit candidate submission. Develop your own concise responses in the original files and verify each final page count. Do not copy fictional events, assign yourself a rubric score from this example, or claim permissions and fieldwork that have not occurred.</p><p>Keep every required prompt and original formatting. Use Arial 11-point text, single spacing, and one-inch margins as directed. Protect identities and obtain required permissions. Check translation provisions, file formats, legibility, and every uploaded file. Upload every required file directly. A hyperlink to a document or video does not count as a submitted upload. Learning examples support understanding, not candidate response editing or clip selection.</p><p><a href=\"#readiness\">Submission preparation</a> &middot; <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Complete official assessment guide</a> &middot; <a href=\"#connections/c2\">Preserved extended Harbor High example</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">Original prompt text and all 35 rubric descriptors are reproduced from the supplied CTC Version 09 materials, copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Line wrapping is normalized. The official originals govern. The older Harbor High example remains a separate teaching case; do not merge its counts or events with Canyon View.</p></section></article>", "search": [{"title": "Cycle 2 Part A: Context, educational focus, and community of practice", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-a", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"A\"><h2 id=\"part-a\">Part A: Context, educational focus, and community of practice</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">1. Investigate | Up to 5 pages; 1 file</p><p>Begin by finding out how adults at the school currently learn together. A community of practice is not simply a committee that meets: its members examine a shared concern, learn through their work, try a response, and revisit evidence. Your leadership task is to make that shared inquiry possible.</p><p>The focus may grow from Cycle 1, but it does not have to. Secure guidance about a suitable educational focus, then form a group whose work can affect that focus. Form a group of 3 to 5 educators or educational partners in addition to yourself. At least 3 of them and you must be seen and heard in each implementation clip.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S1_A_ContextEdFocusCommunityPrac.docx\" download>Download the original Part A template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-A-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-A-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Inquiry into Collaborative Professional Learning</p><h3>1. Describe and analyze the role of collaborative professional learning at your school site/educational setting as it relates to student learning and/or well-being.</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. Provide information about any formal training offered to professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting within the past three years related to collaborative professional learning, communities of practice, or group learning process.</p><p>b. Describe and analyze the ways in which professional educators (e.g., teachers, specialists, aides, support staff) at your school site/educational setting currently engage with one another in professional learning. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of this professional learning in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>c. Describe and analyze the ways in which professional educators engage with one another in communities of practice. Describe the impact and/or lack of impact of the community of practice approach in relation to student learning and/or well-being.</p><p>d. Describe how collaborative professional learning reflects evidence-based adult learning processes and cite relevant research to support your analysis.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address all four subparts. Establish what actually happened during the past three years, distinguish formal training from ongoing collaboration, and analyze evidence of impact or its absence. A meeting schedule proves an opportunity existed, not that adults or students learned.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At Canyon View, Year 1 training introduced common writing criteria, Year 2 sessions practiced scoring, and Year 3 added a monthly inquiry period. Department minutes show that most recent meetings still addressed deadlines. Two teams compared student drafts; neither routinely revisited the same learners after a change in instruction. Teachers report that shared scoring clarified expectations, but the available records do not establish improved student writing. The community model offers repeated examination of practice, while the one-time sessions chiefly introduced information. Research on professional development helps us ask whether teachers have opportunities to practice, receive feedback, and revisit evidence, not merely attend. <a href=\"https://learningpolicyinstitute.org/product/effective-teacher-professional-development-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Darling-Hammond, Hyler, and Gardner (2017)</a> led me to examine opportunities for rehearsal and feedback in our meeting records. I therefore reserved time for teachers to try a model and return with work. The review describes effective programs; it does not establish an effect of our short inquiry.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This distinguishes types of professional learning and the strength of evidence for their effects. Use your actual training records, minutes, work, and participant perspectives. <a href=\"https://learningpolicyinstitute.org/product/effective-teacher-professional-development-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Darling-Hammond, Hyler, and Gardner (2017)</a> identify features of effective professional development; their study does not evaluate this fictional school.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">I. Inquiry into Collaborative Professional Learning</p><h3>2. As a result of your investigation and given the educational setting as well as available data related to student learning and/or well-being, describe the most important issues you will need to consider in preparing to facilitate and support a community of practice to engage in collaborative professional learning.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain what the investigation means for your facilitation. Name a small number of consequential issues and the response each requires.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Beyond the monthly schoolwide inquiry period, this volunteer group has 35 protected minutes every other week. Teachers have no common method for tracking the reasoning in student paragraphs. The ELD teacher reports that language explanations are sometimes mistaken for a lack of ideas. I will reserve protected work-analysis time, agree on a reasoning criterion, and invite language and disability expertise before the group selects a response.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A contextual detail becomes useful when it changes your plan. Do not replace analysis with a list of generic meeting norms.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>1. Describe the agreed-upon area of educational focus for improving student learning and/or well-being (obtained with guidance from one or more school administrators and/or teacher leaders) selected for the group\u2019s professional learning. Be sure to include a discussion of the relevant data that supports the selection of this focus and the connection between the educational focus and the vision, mission, and/or goals in your school site/educational setting.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Establish the broad educational focus with appropriate leadership guidance, evidence, and a verified school goal. Do not preselect the group's later problem or strategy here.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The principal and English teacher leader support examining Grade 9 evidence-based writing. In 24 matched baseline writing samples, 4 of 12 English learners and 8 of 12 students not classified as English learners explain how their evidence supports a claim. The fictional school improvement goal calls for equitable access to grade-level argument writing. These samples justify inquiry; they do not describe every ninth grader or establish a cause.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The focus is writing. The group must still determine the modifiable instructional problem and choose a strategy together.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>2. Provide a list of the job titles for each member of the community of practice group, detailing the specific reason(s) for each member\u2019s inclusion and referencing the area of educational focus.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Identify 3-5 partners in addition to yourself. Explain each person's contribution to this focus, not simply their availability.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The four partners are Chen and Rivera, Grade 9 English teachers who teach the sampled learners; Patel, an ELD teacher who can help interpret language demands and learner strengths; and Morgan, a special education co-teacher who can examine accessibility and accommodations. I facilitate as an instructional coach. Each partner contributes to both analysis and implementation within their assignment.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is five adults including the candidate, not five partners plus an unnamed facilitator. Record actual job titles and reasons without assuming expertise from demographic identity.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>3. Provide a description of each group member\u2019s demographics and how this reflects or is different from your school site/educational setting\u2019s demographics.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe each member's relevant demographics and compare the group with the school. Use information people appropriately provide; do not infer identity from names or appearance.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>In this invented context, Chen identifies as Asian American and speaks English and Mandarin; Rivera as Latina and speaks English and Spanish; Patel as South Asian American and speaks English and Gujarati; Morgan as White and English-speaking. The fictional student population includes substantial Latino, Black, and Asian communities and several home languages. No partner identifies as Black, and the group does not represent every family language. These differences make student perspectives and checks on our assumptions important; no member speaks for an entire group.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Comparison supports an honest account of representation and missing perspectives. Protect identities in actual assessment evidence and avoid turning demographics into explanations of competence.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>4. Provide a brief description of your relationship or positionality (e.g., coach, mentor, teacher colleague, administrator) to each member of the group.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain your relationship to each member and how power or trust may affect participation.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>I am a non-evaluative instructional coach to Chen and Rivera and a collaborating colleague of Patel and Morgan; I do not assign their evaluations. I previously planned a lesson with Chen, so her familiarity with my approach may make her more comfortable speaking first. I will use individual evidence review before discussion and invite interpretations from all four members before proposing a conclusion.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Positionality is your relationship and authority in this particular group. Claiming everyone is equal does not remove differences in status or familiarity.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q7\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Selecting an Area of Educational Focus Based on Data and Establishing a Community of Practice</p><h3>5. Explain how the work of this group is likely to advance conditions for students\u2019 learning and/or well-being in relation to the area of educational focus.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe a plausible pathway from adult learning to improved student conditions, while keeping the result prospective at this stage.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Shared analysis may help teachers distinguish a missing explanation from a language-access barrier. If the group learns to make reasoning visible and reviews who benefits, students may receive more consistent opportunities to explain and revise. We have not implemented a strategy yet; later work samples and meeting records will be needed to judge initial results.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A reasoned expectation is not an accomplished outcome. Name what you will need to observe before claiming progress.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-1\"><h3>Rubric 2.1: Professional learning context</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate describe and analyze the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how do these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being?</p><p>Analyze current professional learning and its relationship to student learning or well-being.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not describe the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. OR Candidate does not analyze how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing description or analysis can leave the purpose unexplained.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate minimally describes the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a vague analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Listing activities is not the same as interpreting their role or impact.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides a clear detailed description of the role of current practices of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting. Candidate provides a clear analysis of how these practices relate to student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Describe and analyze the connection to student learning using the setting's evidence.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains the relationship between different forms of collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting and how these practices have impacted student learning and/or well-being, providing evidence from observations, documentation, surveys, student work, and/or staff consultations.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Compare different forms and the evidence of their impacts.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate critiques collaborative professional learning at their school site/educational setting, referring to evidence-based adult learning processes and/or research that supports or refutes the practices and outlines implications for facilitating a community of practice at their school site/educational setting.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use adult-learning research to critique practice and explain implications.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2B, 2C, 2D</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=15\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 15 (printed page 11)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> Part A compares training, department routines, and inquiry work. The limited impact evidence is named rather than filled with an invented outcome.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-2\"><h3>Rubric 2.2: Focus and membership</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate select an area of educational focus based on student data that corresponds to the vision, mission, and/or goals of their school site/educational setting? How does the candidate choose a group of educators/educational partners to participate in a community of practice in relation to the selected area of educational focus?</p><p>Connect the focus, data, school commitments, membership, and your relationships.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not select or selects an area of educational focus that is not based on student data or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate\u2019s school site/educational setting. OR Candidate provides little detail about demographic characteristics and/or does not provide reasons for member inclusion in the community of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing focus evidence or membership reasoning weakens the foundation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate selects and minimally describes an area of educational focus loosely based on student data and/or the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate\u2019s school site/educational setting. Candidate lists group members and provides unclear or irrelevant details of their demographics and characteristics; rationale for member inclusion in the community of practice is not clear.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Minimal detail leaves the fit of the focus or partners unclear.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate selects an area of educational focus, clearly referring to data on student learning and/or well-being, and explains the connection to the vision, mission, and/or goals of the candidate\u2019s school site/educational setting. Candidate clearly describes community of practice group members by demographic characteristics, current job titles, assignments, and positional relationships and provides clear reasons for each member\u2019s inclusion in the group in relation to the selected area of educational focus.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain the focus and each member's relevant context, role, and relationship.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate articulates how the work of the group is likely to advance conditions for student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain how the group's work may improve student conditions.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides a rationale for including the specific members in their community of practice to ensure diverse viewpoints are represented and respected, leading to supports for all students to learn and to experience a positive sense of well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Show intentional attention to diverse perspectives and student learning or well-being.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Written Narrative: Context, Area of Educational Focus, Community of Practice (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 16 (printed page 12)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The case distinguishes an agreed writing focus from the problem the group later develops. Its demographic account identifies missing perspectives, not a claim of complete representation.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the three-year account compare professional-learning approaches and distinguish evidence of participation from evidence of impact?</li><li>Are the educational focus, school goal, 3-5 partners besides you, relevant perspectives, and your relationship with each partner explained?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-b\">Continue to Part B <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 Part B: Planning meeting agenda", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-b", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"B\"><h2 id=\"part-b\">Part B: Planning meeting agenda</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>The agenda is prepared before the planning meeting. It gives the group a route through evidence, problem definition, strategy selection, and implementation planning. The planning meeting is distinct from the later three implementation meetings.</p><p>An agenda is required; this particular agenda template is optional. A program-supported format may be used if it includes the required information. Allow real time for interpretation and disagreement. A full agenda is not evidence that every item was completed.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_B_PlanningMeetingAgenda_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Download the original Part B template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-B-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-B-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part B template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>1. Review the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan an opening that establishes the shared purpose and its connection to school goals.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Planning meeting, September 9, 3:15-4:00. All four partners attend, facilitated by the candidate (the instructional coach). Norms: examine evidence before explanation, invite each perspective, and record disagreement respectfully. First 5 minutes: connect Grade 9 argument writing to the school's equitable-access goal.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Include date, start/end time, participants and roles, focal group, goals, and norms in the header. An agenda records intended work, not decisions that have already occurred.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>2. Review and analyze data:</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>a. Data source(s):</p><p>b. Discussion of data:</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Name the data and the questions the group will investigate; do not write the conclusion in advance.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 5-18: examine the 24 anonymized baseline paragraphs and the reasoning counts (English learners 4/12; peers not classified as EL 8/12). Compare two scoring disagreements and look at language supports documented in lesson materials. Ask: Where is reasoning visible? Which students demonstrate it orally but not yet in writing? What cannot this sample tell us?</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Both the source and planned discussion belong here. A group rate should lead back to actual work and opportunities to learn.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>3. Identify a problem of practice:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Create room for the group to define the problem from evidence.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 18-25: identify a current teaching practice that may be changed. Each partner proposes an interpretation, identifies supporting work, and names a competing explanation before the group drafts a problem of practice.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The agenda should invite a decision. It should not imply that your preferred problem was collaboratively selected before the meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>4. Addressing the problem of practice:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan a reasoned choice of one evidence-based strategy.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 25-34: compare the fit of a Model-Practice-Reflect writing routine with an approach limited to giving longer written comments. Consider evidence, instructional time, accessibility, and what the selected routine would look like in both English classrooms.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Use research to examine fit, not decorate a predetermined choice. <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/PracticeGuide/22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">WWC, Teaching Secondary Students to Write Effectively</a> supports explicitly teaching writing strategies through a Model-Practice-Reflect cycle.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>5. Implementation plan:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reserve time to decide who will implement, how adults will learn the strategy, and how evidence will be collected.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 34-40: plan a rehearsal using one common paragraph, two classroom uses per week for six weeks, a shared reasoning criterion, and a matched set of student samples at baseline and weeks 2, 4, and 6. Check accommodations and permitted storage.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> An implementation plan needs teachable actions and comparable evidence, not just a target date.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part B</p><h3>6. Next steps and action items:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Close with responsibilities, support, and the next meeting.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Minutes 40-45: confirm owners and due dates, collect unresolved questions, and schedule implementation meetings for September 23, October 7, and October 21, each 3:15-3:50. Ask every partner to state what they will bring to the first meeting.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The next three meetings examine instruction that has actually taken place; they are not three further planning meetings.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the agenda identify date, time, roles, goals, norms, evidence, and genuine decision time?</li><li>Does it leave the problem and strategy open for shared analysis and reserve time for implementation responsibilities?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-c\">Continue to Part C <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 Part C: Planning meeting minutes", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-c", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"C\"><h2 id=\"part-c\">Part C: Planning meeting minutes</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Minutes preserve what happened: the evidence considered, interpretations and differing views, agreements, responsibilities, and next steps. They are neither a word-for-word transcript nor a polished account that erases uncertainty.</p><p>The minutes are required even though the supplied format is optional. Explain what changed from the agenda when that matters. If the group did not reach a decision, record the unfinished work and the follow-up instead of inventing consensus.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_C_PlanningMeetingMinutes_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Download the original Part C template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-C-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>1. Review the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/professional context:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Record what the group actually discussed about the school goal.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>September 9 minutes: Rivera connected the access goal to grade-level reasoning rather than easier assignments. Patel asked that language supports preserve the intellectual demand. The group agreed to examine access to explanations and revision opportunities.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Minutes preserve the reasoning and voices behind agreement. Do not simply copy the agenda into past tense.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>2. Review and analyze data:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Show the group's analysis and the limitations it noticed.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen initially counted three paragraphs with a quotation as successful. Morgan noted that the criterion requires explaining the quotation's relevance. After checking the common criterion together, the group retained the baseline counts of 4/12 English learners and 8/12 peers. Patel noted that two unsuccessful writers had offered relevant oral explanations.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The disagreement matters because it improved interpretation. Counts and examples should agree, and a sample must not be relabeled a schoolwide result.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>3. Identify a problem of practice:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Document the jointly selected, modifiable problem.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The group agreed: Grade 9 writing lessons do not yet provide consistent modeling and supported practice in connecting evidence to a claim, particularly when students are developing the language for that explanation. We will examine the instructional opportunity, not assume students lack motivation.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A problem of practice locates something adults can change. It is more specific than a broad performance gap.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>4. Addressing the problem of practice:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Record the selected strategy and why alternatives were not chosen.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Members selected a Model-Practice-Reflect routine: teachers think aloud while constructing an explanation, students practice with appropriate access supports, and students use the criterion to reflect on their writing. Rivera worried this could become copying; the group added comparison of two different explanations while retaining the routine's purpose. More teacher comments alone would not address the lack of visible modeling.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Consensus means a shared, workable understanding with concerns acknowledged. Silence or a majority vote does not by itself show that everyone understands the decision.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>5. Implementation plan:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Record concrete implementation and monitoring agreements.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen and Rivera will use the routine twice weekly. Patel will co-plan language supports and Morgan will check access to the written and oral directions. The candidate will arrange a 20-minute rehearsal by September 12. The group will use comparable prompts and the same criterion, retain the same 24 sampled learners where possible, and record absences or missing work separately.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The minutes now establish what was decided. The agenda only shows that the topic was planned.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part C</p><h3>6. Next steps and action items:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Capture owners, deadlines, support, and follow-through.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Chen drafts the first model by September 11; Rivera brings a contrasting model; Patel reviews supports by September 12; Morgan checks accessibility the same day. The candidate prepares the tracking sheet and rehearsal. Everyone brings anonymized initial work and a brief implementation log on September 23. Members confirm that no additional grading category is being imposed.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This makes the commitments auditable. Later minutes should show whether the tasks occurred and how unmet commitments were handled.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Do the minutes preserve differing interpretations, the shared problem and strategy, and reasons for the choice?</li><li>Are owners, support, due dates, unresolved questions, and follow-through recorded as decisions actually made?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-d\">Continue to Part D <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 Part D: Problem of practice and selected strategy", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-d", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"D\"><h2 id=\"part-d\">Part D: Problem of practice and selected strategy</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">2. Plan | Up to 4 pages; 1 file</p><p>Now explain the reasoning behind the planning meeting. The agenda and minutes document the event; Part D analyzes how the group used evidence and how your facilitation supported the decisions. Keep the broad educational focus, specific problem of practice, and selected strategy distinct.</p><p>The supplied Word template labels its two sections II and III; the guide's directions refer to the corresponding sections as I and II. The original template labels and prompts are retained here. Match by section title and task, and do not renumber the official template yourself.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S2_D_ProbPracDescStrategy.docx\" download>Download the original Part D template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-D-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-D-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part D template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Collaboratively Select a Problem of Practice Based on Student Data and Data from Your Professional Context</p><h3>1. Describe how you: (a) worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the educational focus; (b) selected a problem of practice based on the area of educational focus; and (c) addressed how practitioners could potentially improve student learning and/or well-being by improving the current practices at your school site/educational setting.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how your facilitation connected group analysis, problem selection, and a change in practice. Refer to evidence of the process rather than announcing the final problem alone.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>I asked members to score independently before discussion. When our judgments differed, we returned to the explanation criterion and the work. This shifted discussion from students' effort to the opportunities teachers provide to model and practice reasoning. The minutes record Patel's oral-language counterexample and the resulting problem statement.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Explain your action, the group's response, and why that response matters. A list of meeting activities leaves the leadership reasoning invisible.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Collaboratively Select a Problem of Practice Based on Student Data and Data from Your Professional Context</p><h3>2. Explain how you worked collaboratively with the group to review your collected data on student demographics, student learning and academic performance or well-being, and other relevant data differentiated by student groups.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how different student groups and relevant contextual evidence were examined together.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>We compared 4/12 and 8/12 baseline results, then examined examples within both groups. Two English learners explained their reasoning orally; four peers also lacked written reasoning. We reviewed lesson materials and noted differences in explicit modeling. We did not conclude that English-learner status caused the pattern or that all English learners needed the same support.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Disaggregation reveals differences but does not explain their causes. Work samples and context help test interpretations without stereotyping.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Collaboratively Select a Problem of Practice Based on Student Data and Data from Your Professional Context</p><h3>3. Describe the ways you facilitated the meeting to acknowledge how the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current practices in your professional context.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Show how you helped the group identify a practice it could realistically change.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>When a member proposed that students should try harder, I asked what students had been shown and what instructional opportunity we controlled. Members identified inconsistent modeling and supported practice. We narrowed the inquiry to a repeatable writing routine within existing lessons, not a new schoolwide program.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The redirect concerns evidence and professional responsibility. Do not invent a conversation to make your actual facilitation sound stronger.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">II. Collaboratively Select a Problem of Practice Based on Student Data and Data from Your Professional Context</p><h3>4. How does the identified problem of practice: (a) correspond to the vision, mission, and/or goals of your school site/educational setting; (b) reflect California state standards and/or curriculum frameworks or equivalent, or other applicable standards; and (c) address existing differences in student group performance or well-being?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Connect the problem to the school commitment, an applicable content standard, and the observed group differences.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The school goal calls for equitable access to grade-level writing. The problem concerns connecting evidence with a claim, one component of <a href=\"https://www2.cde.ca.gov/cacs/id/web/5925\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">California W.9-10.1</a>, which addresses argument writing with reasoned support and appropriate evidence. A single paragraph does not demonstrate the whole standard. The baseline difference is four students in equally sized sampled groups, while unsuccessful work appears in both groups. Our response must maintain the goal and vary access supports.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Verify and cite your actual grade/content standard. A CSTP focus for teaching practice does not replace the student content standard requested here.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">III. Identify a Strategy to Address the Problem of Practice</p><h3>1. Explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting in selecting one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice, potentially leading to improved student learning and/or well-being across student groups. Be sure to include how you facilitated the group to help make their selection.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how the group chose one strategy together, including your facilitation and the rationale for its fit.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>I asked each member to compare a Model-Practice-Reflect routine with longer written comments using the same criteria: the observed need, research, feasibility, and accessibility. Patel and Morgan identified supports needed to make practice usable. Members selected the routine because it makes the reasoning process available before students work independently; they retained Rivera's concern about copying as a monitoring question. We used Recommendation 1 in <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/ncee/wwc/PracticeGuide/22\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">WWC, Teaching Secondary Students to Write Effectively</a> to specify modeling, practice, and reflection. This changed our choice from adding comments after writing to making the reasoning process visible during instruction. The local language supports remain adaptations to monitor.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A research citation cannot establish that a decision was collaborative. Explain both the choice and the process that produced it.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">III. Identify a Strategy to Address the Problem of Practice</p><h3>2. Describe the group\u2019s rationale for selecting this evidence-based strategy. How will the group learn this strategy and how to implement it? What challenges do you anticipate the group might encounter and how could they be addressed?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe adult learning, likely challenges, and specific supports for implementation.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Teachers will rehearse the think-aloud, compare models, and examine student practice together before using the routine. A challenge is that fluent writing may conceal missing reasoning; the common criterion separates the explanation from surface correctness. Time is another constraint, so the model replaces part of an existing lesson rather than adding a separate intervention period.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The research supports the instructional approach, not every local adaptation. Document what is retained, what is adapted, and why the adaptation should still address the problem.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q7\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">III. Identify a Strategy to Address the Problem of Practice</p><h3>3. What improvements for student learning and/or well-being related to the problem of practice do the group expect the selected evidence-based strategy will yield?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>State the expected improvement as an expectation that can be examined.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>We expect more sampled students to explain how evidence supports their claim, with attention to progress among English learners and students who initially struggle in either group. We also expect teachers to make the criterion more explicit. We will not equate participation, completed worksheets, or more meetings with improved reasoning.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Keep the outcome connected to the learning problem. Establish how it will be recognized before reviewing results.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q8\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">III. Identify a Strategy to Address the Problem of Practice</p><h3>4. How will you facilitate the group in determining a monitoring process to understand the impact of the evidence-based strategy? Describe how you and the group plan to monitor and analyze initial implementation.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Describe a monitoring process the group helped design.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At weeks 2, 4, and 6, the group will score comparable paragraphs using the baseline criterion and review disagreements with the actual work. We will preserve student sample IDs, note missing work, separate the two group counts, and review implementation logs. Each meeting will consider successful cases, persistent difficulties, and whether an adaptation is needed.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Comparable does not mean identical. Record task differences and absences, and avoid treating a small descriptive inquiry as a causal evaluation.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-3\"><h3>Rubric 2.3: Collaborative problem definition</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: Using the agreed-upon area of educational focus as a starting point, how does the candidate work collaboratively with the group to review data sets and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being? How does the candidate work with the group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be addressed with a change in current instructional practice at their school site/educational setting?</p><p>Demonstrate the group's role in analyzing data and selecting a problem of practice.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not involve the group in analyzing data sets to select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. OR Candidate provides no evidence that they worked collaboratively with the group to select the problem of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A problem selected without the group or evidence is not collaborative inquiry.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate minimally involves the group in the analysis of data sets and/or the selection of the problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate minimally involves the group in the selection of the problem of practice and/or minimally describes how the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Participation or data use may be too limited to establish shared reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly explains how they worked collaboratively with the group to analyze student data sets on the selected educational focus and select a problem of practice related to student learning and/or well-being. Candidate clearly describes how they worked collaboratively with group members to acknowledge that the problem of practice might be mitigated by a change of current practices at their school site/educational setting.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain shared analysis, the selected problem, and the practice that can change.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate differentiates the academic performance for more than one student group and/or issue of well-being, providing supporting student data/evidence and explains how they facilitated the group to collaboratively address these differences in defining the problem of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Differentiate needs across more than one student group with evidence.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains in detail both how they facilitated the collaborative process with the group members to analyze student data/evidence and why specific facilitation skills were used.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Analyze how and why your facilitation advanced the group's reasoning.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1C CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2D CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5B, 5C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 20 (printed page 16)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The baseline counts, oral-language counterexample, and scoring disagreement show how the inquiry moved toward instructional opportunity.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-4\"><h3>Rubric 2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy to address the selected problem of practice? How does the candidate describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being?</p><p>Explain collaborative selection and the strategy's potential effect on the identified need.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not explain the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate does not describe the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A strategy without joint selection or a relevant rationale is insufficient.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate vaguely explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate minimally discusses the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A vague choice leaves the expected benefit unexplained.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly explains the collaborative process used during the planning meeting to select one relevant evidence-based strategy that addresses the problem of practice. Candidate clearly describes the potential impact of the strategy on student learning and/or well-being.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Explain the process, evidence base, and potential effect.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate describes in detail how the collaboratively determined evidence-based strategy will have a positive impact on student learning and/or well-being across specific student groups.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Specify how different student groups may benefit.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate thoroughly explains potential implementation implications and/or challenges across multiple student groups and suggests feasible next steps for each group, as appropriate.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Anticipate implementation challenges across groups and feasible responses.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Planning Meeting Agenda (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part C: Planning Meeting Minutes (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part D: Written Narrative: Identifying a Problem of Practice and a Strategy to Address It (up to 4 pages) CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 21 (printed page 17)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The routine has a research basis, but local adaptations and actual benefits still require examination. Research is not a guarantee for Canyon View.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Can the reader trace the problem to the group analysis and the strategy to research and the identified need?</li><li>Have you explained your facilitation, likely implementation challenges, expected benefits across groups, and collaborative monitoring within four pages?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-e\">Continue to Part E <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 Part E: Implementation meeting agendas", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-e", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"E\"><h2 id=\"part-e\">Part E: Implementation meeting agendas</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | At least 3 meetings; all agendas in 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Implementation means the strategy is actually used in practice over several weeks. Each implementation meeting examines new evidence from that use. Three meetings about what teachers might do later are not a substitute.</p><p>Plan at least three implementation meetings and video-record each one. Keep dates, start/end times, grade level, participants and roles, norms, goals, the named strategy, new evidence, a summary process, and next steps visible. These optional agenda layouts repeat because each meeting needs its own plan.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_E_AgendasImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Download the original Part E template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 1 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 1 of 3. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-E-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 1 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 1 of 3. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 2 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 2 of 3. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-E-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 2 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 2 of 3. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 3 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 3 of 3. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-E-3.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 3 of 3. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part E template, page 3 of 3. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 1</p><h3>1. Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan what the group will examine after instruction, including successful and difficult cases.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Meeting 1, September 23, 3:15-3:50, Grade 9; same four partners and the candidate as instructional coach. Norms remain evidence first and all voices before a conclusion. Bring the first two weeks of work and the September 9 planning commitments. Examine whether the rehearsed models made reasoning visible, and whether students are explaining rather than copying.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The agenda carries forward a known decision and an open question. New findings belong in the minutes after this meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 1</p><h3>2. Summary of the meeting:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reserve time for members to check the accuracy of the shared summary.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minute 25 of Meeting 1, ask members to state the strongest supported conclusion, a limitation, and any disagreement that should remain in the record.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is a planned summary process. The agreed conclusion belongs in the minutes after the meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 1</p><h3>3. Next steps for the PLC:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan a close that establishes action, support, and the next meeting.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minutes 30-35 of Meeting 1, confirm who will do what, the due date, any support required, and the next meeting date. Ask whether the proposed action responds to the reviewed learning evidence.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A recurring heading can remain the same while the evidence and decisions change. Reusing identical completed agendas without new data obscures implementation.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 2</p><h3>1. Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan what the group will examine after instruction, including successful and difficult cases.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Meeting 2, October 7, 3:15-3:50, Grade 9; same four partners and the candidate as instructional coach. Norms remain evidence first and all voices before a conclusion. Revisit the September 23 decision to compare contrasting explanations. Bring the models completed by September 25 and the new matched paragraphs. Ask whether the contrast clarified reasoning and which learners still need another way to rehearse their ideas.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The agenda carries forward a known decision and an open question. New findings belong in the minutes after this meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 2</p><h3>2. Summary of the meeting:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reserve time for members to check the accuracy of the shared summary.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minute 25 of Meeting 2, ask members what the new work suggests about the contrasting models, which learners still need support, and what disagreements remain about the sentence frame.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is a planned summary process. The agreed conclusion belongs in the minutes after the meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q6\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 2</p><h3>3. Next steps for the PLC:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan a close that establishes action, support, and the next meeting.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minutes 30-35 of Meeting 2, agree on the next adjustment in light of the evidence just reviewed. Consider whether oral rehearsal is worth trying while keeping the writing criterion unchanged; assign responsibilities and identify work to bring on October 21.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A recurring heading can remain the same while the evidence and decisions change. Reusing identical completed agendas without new data obscures implementation.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q7\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 3</p><h3>1. Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan what the group will examine after instruction, including successful and difficult cases.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Meeting 3, October 21, 3:15-3:50, Grade 9; same four partners and the candidate as instructional coach. Norms remain evidence first and all voices before a conclusion. Revisit the October 7 oral-rehearsal adjustment. Bring initial and revised pairs and notes on who used the support. Ask whether the written explanation meets the unchanged criterion, including cases that remain difficult.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> The agenda carries forward a known decision and an open question. New findings belong in the minutes after this meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q8\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 3</p><h3>2. Summary of the meeting:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Reserve time for members to check the accuracy of the shared summary.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minute 25 of Meeting 3, ask members what the week-6 work supports, which learning needs remain, and what this small sample cannot show about schoolwide effectiveness.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is a planned summary process. The agreed conclusion belongs in the minutes after the meeting.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"e-q9\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Implementation Meeting Agenda 3</p><h3>3. Next steps for the PLC:</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Plan a close that establishes action, support, and the next meeting.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>At minutes 30-35 of Meeting 3, decide whether further inquiry or a wider trial is warranted. Agree on the evidence needed, responsibilities, support, and a follow-up date before closing.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A recurring heading can remain the same while the evidence and decisions change. Reusing identical completed agendas without new data obscures implementation.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are there agendas for at least three implementation meetings after the planning meeting, combined in one file?</li><li>Does each agenda carry forward the previous decision and identify new work and unresolved questions without predicting findings?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-f\">Continue to Part F <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 Part F: Implementation meeting minutes", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-f", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"F\"><h2 id=\"part-f\">Part F: Implementation meeting minutes</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | At least 3 meetings; all minutes in 1 file; no page maximum specified</p><p>Use the minutes to connect one meeting to the next. What was tried after the last decision? What evidence returned? What did members conclude or question? What support did you agree to provide?</p><p>The original format asks what is going well, what needs adjustment, participant reflections, a summary, and next steps. Document actual agreed support to individuals and the group. The fictional records below illustrate those fields; they do not replace your own meeting records.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_F_MinutesImplemenationMeetings_OptionalTemplate.docx\" download>Download the original Part F template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-F-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-F-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part F template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><h3>Original meeting-record fields</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>Implementation Meeting 1: Minutes</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a. What is going well?</p><p>b. What needs adjustment?</p><p>c. PLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p><p>Implementation Meeting 2: Minutes</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a. What is going well?</p><p>b. What needs adjustment?</p><p>c. PLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p><p>Implementation Meeting 3: Minutes</p><p>Early returns/new data based on the implementation of the named strategy:</p><p>a. What is going well?</p><p>b. What needs adjustment?</p><p>c. PLC participant reflections:</p><p>Summary of the meeting:</p><p>Next steps for the PLC:</p></div><h3>Three connected fictional meeting records</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Meeting and findings</th><th scope=\"col\">Discussion and decision</th><th scope=\"col\">Support and follow-through</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Meeting and findings\"><strong>1: September 23, 3:15-3:50</strong><br>15/24; EL 6/12, peers 9/12. Students identify a claim more consistently, but three sampled explanations merely repeat evidence.</td><td data-label=\"Discussion and decision\">Patel notices that a frame helps students begin; Rivera worries it masks missing reasoning. Compare two different explanations in the next model; keep the reasoning criterion unchanged.</td><td data-label=\"Support and follow-through\">Chen and Rivera prepare models by September 25; Patel checks language access and Morgan checks accessibility; coach hosts a 15-minute rehearsal.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Meeting and findings\"><strong>2: October 7, 3:15-3:50</strong><br>18/24; EL 8/12, peers 10/12. Several revised paragraphs now explain relevance; six still do not meet the criterion.</td><td data-label=\"Discussion and decision\">Morgan reports that an oral rehearsal helped one learner; Chen asks how to distinguish the support from lowering the target. Retain the routine and permit appropriate oral rehearsal before written revision; examine the resulting writing against the same criterion.</td><td data-label=\"Support and follow-through\">Teachers retain initial and revised work; Patel and Morgan co-plan supports by October 9; coach assembles matched evidence for October 21.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Meeting and findings\"><strong>3: October 21, 3:15-3:50</strong><br>19/24; EL 9/12, peers 10/12. Progress is uneven and five students still need support; the later gain is smaller.</td><td data-label=\"Discussion and decision\">Rivera warns that newer topics may affect results; all members request individual-case review before expansion. Continue a short inquiry with those learning needs; do not announce schoolwide effectiveness from this sample.</td><td data-label=\"Support and follow-through\">Teachers bring two contrasting cases in two weeks; specialists plan supports; coach arranges time and invites written feedback on the inquiry.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>All five adults attended these fictional meetings. The October 7 minutes record completion of the contrasting models agreed on September 23; the October 21 minutes record review of the initial and revised work requested October 7. If your actual attendance or follow-through differs, record the difference. Do not fill a gap with a statement that the template seems to expect.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Do the minutes cover all three implementation meetings and distinguish findings, interpretations, decisions, and member reflections?</li><li>Do later records show whether earlier commitments occurred, with owners, dates, and support when plans changed?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-g\">Continue to Part G <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 Part G: Collaborative group work products", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-g", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"G\"><h2 id=\"part-g\">Part G: Collaborative group work products</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | Up to 10 pages; 1 file</p><p>Submit selected products of the community's collaborative work, not only a narrative saying the group made something. A common scoring tool, joint analysis, annotated instructional model, or monitoring document may make the learning process visible when it genuinely came from this group.</p><p>Choose products that explain decisions and progress. Label each with its purpose and when the group used it. Protect identities. A stack of unrelated worksheets is less informative than a small, coherent set that connects the problem, strategy, and results.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/guide-part-G.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part G. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><h3>What the products themselves can show</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Fictional product</th><th scope=\"col\">Excerpt from the product</th><th scope=\"col\">Why the group retained it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Fictional product\">G1: Common reasoning criterion</td><td data-label=\"Excerpt from the product\">Meets: explains why a relevant piece of evidence supports the claim. Not yet: quotes or restates evidence without explaining its connection. Surface correctness is examined separately.</td><td data-label=\"Why the group retained it\">Agreed at planning; reused at all three implementation meetings.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Fictional product\">G2: Joint model annotation</td><td data-label=\"Excerpt from the product\">Model A: \"The character returns the wallet. This shows responsibility because the action protects another person's belongings even when no one is watching.\" Annotation: the because clause explains why the evidence supports the claim. Model B only repeats that the wallet was returned.</td><td data-label=\"Why the group retained it\">Members contrast reasoning with repetition; the language support should not replace the intellectual work.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Fictional product\">G3: Matched monitoring record</td><td data-label=\"Excerpt from the product\">Baseline: EL 4/12, peers 8/12, total 12/24. Week 2: 6/12, 9/12, 15/24. Week 4: 8/12, 10/12, 18/24. Week 6: 9/12, 10/12, 19/24. No missing sampled work in this invented dataset.</td><td data-label=\"Why the group retained it\">Comparable prompts and one criterion support descriptive comparison. Topic, instruction, and time remain possible influences.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Fictional product\">G4: Joint adjustment record</td><td data-label=\"Excerpt from the product\">October 7: retain modeling, practice, and reflection; allow appropriate oral rehearsal, then judge the written explanation using the same criterion. Review initial/revised pairs October 21.</td><td data-label=\"Why the group retained it\">Connects feedback, a decision, implementation, and a later review.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These text excerpts are fictional examples of artifacts, not evidence that a real group produced them. In your own Part G, include the actual selected products within the ten-page total.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are these actual products of this group, labeled by purpose and use, rather than a narrative describing absent artifacts?</li><li>Does the single combined file fit ten pages and connect the products to the meetings, clips, and commentary?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-h\">Continue to Part H <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 Part H: Three implementation meeting video clips", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-h", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"H\"><h2 id=\"part-h\">Part H: Three implementation meeting video clips</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | Exactly 3 video files; up to 5 minutes each</p><p>Record all implementation meetings so authentic facilitation and changes over time can be examined. Plan consent, camera placement, and audio beforehand. The candidate and at least three partners must be seen and heard as directed. The planning meeting is a separate event and is not the source of these required implementation clips.</p><p>Select three clips addressing the official functions below. The guide does not require one clip from each meeting, but all three implementation meetings and their agendas/minutes remain required. Follow the guide's editing directions and use only the minimum editing needed to meet the specified constructs and limits; do not stage or script the assessed interaction.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p>There is no separate official blank template for this part in the supplied materials. This is an image of the official guide instructions, not a newly invented template.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/guide-part-H.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 guide instructions for Part H. This is a guide page, not a blank template.</figcaption></figure><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Official clip function</th><th scope=\"col\">Illustrative selection</th><th scope=\"col\">What must be visible</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Official clip function\">Clip 1: Setting the Purpose and Data Review</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative selection\">4:30 from implementation meeting 1</td><td data-label=\"What must be visible\">Purpose and problem of practice; members examining implementation evidence, not the facilitator reading a report alone.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Official clip function\">Clip 2: Professional Collaboration</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative selection\">4:40 from implementation meeting 2</td><td data-label=\"What must be visible\">Contributions, differing perspectives, feedback, focus, and your facilitation of shared reasoning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Official clip function\">Clip 3: Results and Next Steps</td><td data-label=\"Illustrative selection\">4:30 from implementation meeting 3</td><td data-label=\"What must be visible\">Joint interpretation of initial results and agreement about next steps.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The example durations all fit the five-minute individual maximum. They are planning illustrations, not actual media files. A total below 15 minutes does not excuse one clip exceeding 5 minutes. Keep original footage in authorized storage and inspect the uploaded files, not just the editing project.</p><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are there exactly three authentic implementation clips, each no longer than five minutes, addressing the three named functions?</li><li>Can the candidate and at least three partners be seen and heard as required, with consent and usable sound, and are all implementation meetings recorded?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-i\">Continue to Part I <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 Part I: Commentary on implementation meeting clips", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-i", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"I\"><h2 id=\"part-i\">Part I: Commentary on implementation meeting clips</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">3. Act | Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>The video shows an event. Commentary explains the leadership reasoning the event alone may not reveal. For each relevant moment, identify the submitted clip number and a precise timestamp, explain what you did, why, and the impact supported by the group's response.</p><p>Address all five prompts at least once across the three clips. Do not give the entire five-minute clip as the timestamp for every claim. Commentary that describes an event absent from the submitted clips does not become evidence because the prose is persuasive.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S3_I_CommentaryImplementationMeetings.docx\" download>Download the original Part I template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-I-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-I-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part I template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>1. Describe how you set the purpose for the meetings/reviewed the problem of practice with the group.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Connect a specific video moment to the meeting purpose. Explain why the action helped the group work, not only what you announced.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 1, 00:15-00:55: I returned to the problem of inconsistent opportunities to practice reasoning and asked members to identify the work that would let us examine progress. Chen brought the matched sample table into the discussion. This kept the group focused on learning rather than the number of lessons completed.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> This is a model of commentary structure, not a real clip. In your submission the moment, response, and timestamp must be visible or audible in the actual submitted file.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>2. Describe how you and the community of practice evaluated data from the implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain how you and the group interpreted initial implementation evidence.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 1, 01:10-03:20: members compare baseline 12/24 with week-2 15/24, then examine one paragraph without reasoning. I ask which improvement is visible and what remains uncertain. Patel identifies that a sentence frame helped participation but did not always produce an explanation. The group distinguishes increased completion from the target reasoning.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> An improved total is not sufficient analysis. Explain which evidence supported the conclusion and which evidence challenged it.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>3. Describe any agreements or decisions made by the group and how you facilitated the community of practice to arrive at a consensual understanding of any initial implementation results.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain the shared understanding reached and how you helped members reach it.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 2, 00:45-02:10: Rivera questions whether the frame encourages copying. I invite a comparison of two student responses before summarizing a possible agreement. Members agree to retain the routine, compare contrasting models, and let students explain orally before revising when appropriate. Each member states the implication for their role. This is October 7, meeting 2: Rivera is revisiting the copying concern and contrasting-model adjustment discussed September 23, not first proposing it. The new decision is appropriate oral rehearsal, recorded in the meeting-2 minutes and product G4 for review October 21.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Agreement should be grounded in the recorded discussion. A facilitator's summary alone does not prove consensual understanding.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>4. Describe how you</h3><div class=\"official\"><p>valued and respected diverse viewpoints,</p><p>responded to individual and collective feedback,</p><p>maintained the group\u2019s focus and energy, and</p><p>monitored progress toward the goals of the meetings.</p></div><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Address every bullet: differing views, feedback, energy and focus, and progress. Tie each claim to an observable action.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 2, 02:15-04:20: I invite Morgan's access concern before finalizing the decision, acknowledge Rivera's criticism of the model, and ask the group to compare both with the work. I return to the reasoning criterion when discussion turns to handwriting, then check the agreed action against the meeting goal. Patel adds a support that the initial proposal omitted.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Respect is demonstrated by what happens to a contribution, not merely by saying everyone was valued. Select actual evidence; do not claim all four bullets from a moment that only shows one.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"i-q5\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part I</p><h3>5. Describe how you collaborated with the group to determine any next steps for implementation of the selected evidence-based strategy.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Show a joint decision about what happens after the meeting, including support and responsibility.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Illustrative Clip 3, 02:00-04:10: after reviewing week-6 results of 19/24, members identify five learners still needing support. Chen proposes a further short cycle and Rivera asks for time to compare individual work. We agree that teachers will bring two contrasting samples, Patel and Morgan will co-plan appropriate supports, and I will schedule a follow-up in two weeks.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A next step belongs to the group, with named responsibilities and a reason from the findings. It is not simply your recommendation to continue.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-5\"><h3>Rubric 2.5: Facilitation of group learning</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate demonstrate and analyze a variety of their facilitation skills of group learning\u2014 including documenting group decisions, supporting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy\u2014 while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, feedback, and progress during the implementation meetings?</p><p>Demonstrate and analyze facilitation, decisions, diverse perspectives, and focus.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not demonstrate facilitation skills during the implementation meetings. OR Candidate\u2019s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group learning or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">A summary of a meeting is not an analysis of demonstrated facilitation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate demonstrates minimal facilitation skills that are vaguely supportive of group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and/or maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate\u2019s commentary does not clearly provide an analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Minimal evidence or vague commentary leaves actions and effects unclear.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly demonstrates facilitation skills that support group learning during the implementation meetings while facilitating a collaborative process of group consensus, including documenting group decisions, respecting diverse viewpoints, and maintaining group focus and energy. Candidate\u2019s commentary provides a clear analysis (what they did and why) of their facilitation of group learning.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Show the actions and explain their purpose and visible impact.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate purposefully seeks diverse viewpoints and clearly encourages all members of the community to speak and share ideas during meetings.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Intentionally invite contributions from every member.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate uses their commentary to critique their facilitation skills used during the implementation meetings based on group learning processes and/or research that supports facilitation practices.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use relevant research to critique your own facilitation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) \u2022 Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) \u2022 Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 27 (printed page 23)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The illustrative moments show what commentary can analyze. They do not constitute the candidate's real video evidence.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-6\"><h3>Rubric 2.6: Results and next steps</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate facilitate a discussion that leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results? How does the candidate collaboratively lead the group in jointly determining next steps?</p><p>Show shared interpretation of results and collaborative next steps.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not facilitate a discussion about implementation results. OR Next steps are not determined. OR Candidate\u2019s commentary does not provide an analysis of their facilitation of group discussion or is a summary of what was observable in the video.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Missing results, joint next steps, or analysis leaves the inquiry incomplete.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate facilitates a discussion that does not clearly lead to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. It is not clear that the candidate works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine the next steps. Candidate\u2019s commentary provides a vague analysis of their facilitation of group discussions.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Unclear consensus or one-sided decisions weaken the account.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate facilitates a discussion that clearly leads to a consensual understanding of initial implementation results. Candidate clearly works collaboratively with the group to jointly determine next steps. Candidate\u2019s commentary provides a clear analysis of their facilitation of the group discussions.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Demonstrate shared understanding and analyze the process.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate consistently addresses initial results and next steps to inform the collaborative implementation process with group members both within and between meetings.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Show follow-through within and between meetings.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate collaborates with the group to adjust the implementation strategy as needed in response to its challenges or successes.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Show joint adaptation in response to successes and challenges.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Sources of Evidence: \u2022 Part E: Agendas for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part F: Minutes for Implementation Meetings (for at least 3 meetings) (use optional template or locally provided format) \u2022 Part G: Key Collaborative Work Products (up to 10 pages) \u2022 Part H: 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (up to 5 minutes each) \u2022 Part I: Commentary for the 3 Video Clips from the Implementation Meetings (written, up to 8 pages; OR up to 10 minutes of verbal or ASL commentary) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1B CAPE Standard 2; Elements 2A, 2C</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 28 (printed page 24)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> Three rounds of matched work and minutes connect decisions over time. The group does not call a small descriptive pattern proof that the routine caused improvement.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Are all five prompts addressed across the three clips, with precise timestamps checked against the submitted files?</li><li>Does commentary explain your action, purpose, and supported effect, within eight written pages OR ten minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/part-j\">Continue to Part J <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 Part J: Reflective narrative", "url": "#cycle/c2/part-j", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\" data-part=\"J\"><h2 id=\"part-j\">Part J: Reflective narrative</h2><p class=\"eyebrow\">4. Reflect | Up to 4 written pages OR 5 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</p><p>Reflection asks what the process and initial results reveal about your leadership. Use participant feedback and the evidence from this cycle to examine your contribution, not to declare that the group was successful because meetings occurred.</p><p>You can acknowledge mixed results and still analyze strong professional learning. Equitable facilitation includes whose knowledge shaped decisions, how disagreement was handled, and whether your support made meaningful participation possible.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Use your own authentic evidence and keep plans, completed actions, and interpretations distinct. The completion checks are learning aids; the official prompts and rubrics govern.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_S4_J_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Download the original Part J template</a>. The original pages below retain the source wording. The explanations following them are teaching guidance, not replacement prompts.</p><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button class=\"c1-image-expand\" type=\"button\" data-figure-expand data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 2 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge original page\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 2 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle2/templates/part-J-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 2 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Cycle 2 Part J template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.</figcaption></figure><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q1\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>1. Refer to specific evidence (including agendas, minutes, group work products, implementation data, videos, and/or participant feedback) to analyze how your leadership actions influenced the community of practice\u2019s progress toward its goal. Reflect on initial implementation results and feedback from the group to evaluate your strengths and areas for growth as a facilitator. Make explicit connections between the evidence, your leadership skills, equitable facilitation practices, and your next steps for professional growth.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Use results and group feedback to analyze your facilitation, linking evidence to a strength, a growth need, and a next step.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>Matched samples improved from 12/24 to 19/24 meeting the reasoning criterion; English learners moved from 4/12 to 9/12 and peers from 8/12 to 10/12. This supports a description of initial progress, not proof of causation. The October 7 (Meeting 2, week 4) minutes show that inviting disagreement led to a useful adjustment. Members valued the work comparison but said my first agenda allowed too little time for interpretation. I need to plan discussion around the evidence, not fill every minute with tasks.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Results concern students; reflection concerns your leadership contribution and limitations. Both matter, and one cannot substitute for the other.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q2\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>2. Refer to specific evidence (including the agendas and minutes, group work products and initial implementation results, videos and commentary, and/or group feedback on the process) of how effectively you maintained a high standard of professionalism, integrity, and equity (e.g., respect for diverse viewpoints) as you worked with members of the community of practice.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain professionalism, integrity, and equity through specific choices across the process.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>I retained the unsuccessful samples alongside improving work, protected identities, and did not report a missing sample as a failure. The planning minutes and Clip 2 show that Patel's and Morgan's concerns changed the plan. I asked members to critique the routine even when I had advocated it. However, Chen spoke first in two meetings; future facilitation will vary the order and invite written interpretations before discussion.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> An honest limitation can support analysis. General claims of fairness are less useful than evidence of whose ideas influenced decisions and where participation still needs improvement.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q3\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>3. What aspects of school culture and context influenced the facilitation of a community of practice at your school site/educational setting? What actions did you take to respond to these contextually based challenges or supports?</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Explain the effect of culture and context, including your response.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>The protected biweekly period made sustained inquiry possible, but a history of deadline-driven meetings made some members expect announcements rather than joint work. I sent anonymized samples beforehand and used the first five minutes to revisit the inquiry purpose. When an assembly interrupted one lesson, the group recorded the implementation difference instead of pretending all classrooms received the same opportunity.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> Context is not background decoration. Show how it changed your choices and what effect that response had.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"j-q4\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Part J</p><h3>4. Based on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 2, identify specific professional learning goals and describe your next steps for growth.</h3><h4>What this asks you to explain</h4><p>Set a specific professional learning goal from your analysis, with a way to examine growth.</p><h4>Fictional worked example</h4><blockquote><p>During the next six-week inquiry, I will collect individual interpretations before group discussion, track whose contributions change decisions, and ask members whether they had meaningful influence. I will review two recordings with my mentor and compare the decision records. Research on active, collaborative professional learning will help me examine whether my facilitation supports adult reasoning rather than only orderly participation.</p></blockquote><p><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> A goal such as be a better leader is too broad. Name the practice, timeframe, evidence, and professional learning resource; do not claim a research-informed change has already been tested.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-2-7\"><h3>Rubric 2.7: Reflection on facilitation</h3><p class=\"official\">Essential Question: How does the candidate reflect upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills and practices to make specific connections to their strengths and areas for growth, and identify next steps, referring to specific evidence from any of the four steps? How does the candidate demonstrate a variety of leadership skills used to equitably facilitate a community of practice?</p><p>Analyze equitable facilitation, strengths, growth, and professional next steps.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">What to notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides no connection to initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make specific connections to their strengths or areas for growth, or next steps related to leadership practices. OR Candidate does not include specific evidence from any of the cycle\u2019s four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. OR Candidate does not describe their leadership practice using an equitable leadership approach to facilitate a community of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Unsupported reflection, missing results or feedback, or no equity connection creates gaps.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate vaguely or inaccurately reflects upon initial implementation results and/or feedback from the group to make connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and/or next steps. Candidate vaguely refers to evidence from any of the cycle\u2019s four steps to support their reflections and/or inform their analysis. Candidate vaguely demonstrates their capacity to facilitate a community of practice. It is not clear that the candidate has an equitable approach to leadership practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Vague references do not show how evidence supports the judgment.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly reflects upon initial implementation results and feedback from the group to analyze their leadership skills, making specific connections to their strengths, areas for growth, and next steps. Candidate clearly refers to specific, appropriate evidence from any of the four steps to support their reflections and inform their analysis. Candidate clearly demonstrates how their leadership practice equitably facilitated a community of practice.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Make explicit evidence-to-leadership-to-growth connections.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides a deep analysis that illustrates how they maintained professionalism and integrity as well as employed an equitable leadership approach consistently throughout the community of practice inquiry process.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Analyze professionalism, integrity, and equity across the inquiry.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate consults evidence-based practices and/or research related to equitable leadership development, adult learning, and/or group facilitation, then uses the information to analyze their own leadership practices and set professional goals during this cycle.</td><td data-label=\"What to notice\">Use relevant research to analyze your leadership and set goals.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class=\"source-note official\">Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part J: Reflective Narrative (written, up to 4 pages; OR up to 5 minutes of verbal or ASL explanation) CAPE Standard 2; Element 2C CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5B</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official guide, PDF page 31 (printed page 27)</a></p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\"><strong>In the fictional example:</strong> The reflection uses member criticism as evidence for change. A self-selected example cannot establish an assessor score.</p></section><section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does reflection use initial results and partner feedback to explain equitable facilitation, strengths, and growth?</li><li>Is the next professional-learning goal specific and supported by the analysis, within four pages OR five minutes verbal/ASL?</li></ul></section></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 2 limits and rubric map", "url": "#cycle/c2/limits", "html": "<section id=\"limits\"><h2>What you submit, and the limits to plan around</h2><p>Read this before arranging meetings or collecting evidence. These are maxima, not targets. The original narrative template counts toward its page limit. N/A in the guide means no page or time maximum is specified for that part; it does not remove the evidence requirement.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"c1-limits\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Limit and files</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Context, educational focus, and community of practice</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 5 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Planning meeting agenda</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Planning meeting minutes</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Problem of practice and selected strategy</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 4 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">E</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Implementation meeting agendas</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">At least 3 meetings; all agendas in 1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">F</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Implementation meeting minutes</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">At least 3 meetings; all minutes in 1 file; no page maximum specified</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">G</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Collaborative group work products</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 10 pages; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">H</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Three implementation meeting video clips</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Exactly 3 video files; up to 5 minutes each</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">I</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Commentary on implementation meeting clips</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 8 written pages OR 10 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Reflective narrative</td><td data-label=\"Limit and files\">Up to 4 written pages OR 5 minutes verbal/ASL; 1 file</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Agendas and minutes are required even when their sample templates are optional. At least three implementation meetings occur over several weeks, after the separate planning meeting, with real implementation and initial evidence between meetings.</p><p>Part H is three clips of up to five minutes each. Part I's ten-minute alternative is commentary, not extra meeting footage. Part J's spoken alternative is five minutes, not Cycle 3's six. Written and verbal/ASL options are alternatives, not combined allowances.</p><p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C2_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=34\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Original submission specifications</a></p></section><section id=\"foundation\"><h2>Understand the work before filling in the templates</h2><h3>What makes this collaboration?</h3><p>Partners contribute evidence, question interpretations, influence the problem and strategy, implement agreed work, and help decide what findings mean. Your facilitation may include questions, summaries, time management, disagreement, and support between meetings. Explain how those actions affected the inquiry. Participation is not equitable merely because everyone was invited.</p><h3>Keep three decisions distinct</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Decision</th><th scope=\"col\">When and with whom</th><th scope=\"col\">Canyon View example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Educational focus</td><td data-label=\"When and with whom\">Initial investigation, with administrator or teacher-leader guidance</td><td data-label=\"Canyon View example\">Grade 9 evidence-based writing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">Problem of practice</td><td data-label=\"When and with whom\">Planning meeting, jointly from relevant data</td><td data-label=\"Canyon View example\">Inconsistent instructional opportunities to model and practice explaining why evidence supports a claim.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Decision\">One evidence-based strategy</td><td data-label=\"When and with whom\">Planning meeting, jointly considering fit and research</td><td data-label=\"Canyon View example\">A Model-Practice-Reflect writing routine.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Then distinguish planning from implementation: the planning meeting decides what to try; the later meetings examine what was tried and what was learned. An agenda is an intention, minutes are a record, a work product is an artifact, a clip shows interaction, commentary analyzes it, and reflection examines your leadership across the cycle.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-map\"><h2>How the parts and rubrics fit together</h2><p>Each row names one rubric. Read its purpose now, then return to the complete five-level rubric beside the relevant work. A higher level includes the stated earlier levels; extra pages, polished language, or more charts alone do not establish it.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table class=\"c1-overview-map\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Rubric focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">1. Investigate</td><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Understand the setting before convening the group.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-1\"><strong>2.1: Professional learning context</strong></a><br>Analyze current professional learning and its relationship to student learning or well-being.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">1. Investigate</td><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Choose a justified focus and a purposeful group.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-2\"><strong>2.2: Focus and membership</strong></a><br>Connect the focus, data, school commitments, membership, and your relationships.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">2. Plan</td><td data-label=\"Part\">B-D</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Turn shared evidence into a changeable problem.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-3\"><strong>2.3: Collaborative problem definition</strong></a><br>Demonstrate the group's role in analyzing data and selecting a problem of practice.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">2. Plan</td><td data-label=\"Part\">B-D</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Agree on one strategy and a way to learn from it.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-4\"><strong>2.4: Collaborative strategy selection</strong></a><br>Explain collaborative selection and the strategy's potential effect on the identified need.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">E-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Make group reasoning visible in authentic meetings.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-5\"><strong>2.5: Facilitation of group learning</strong></a><br>Demonstrate and analyze facilitation, decisions, diverse perspectives, and focus.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">3. Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">E-I</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Use initial findings to determine what happens next.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-6\"><strong>2.6: Results and next steps</strong></a><br>Show shared interpretation of results and collaborative next steps.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">4. Reflect</td><td data-label=\"Part\">J</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Learn from the results and the group's feedback.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c2/rubric-2-7\"><strong>2.7: Reflection on facilitation</strong></a><br>Analyze equitable facilitation, strengths, growth, and professional next steps.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section id=\"fictional-case\"><h2>Fictional Example Case</h2><p class=\"source-note\">All school context, people, meetings, work samples, quotations, counts, and outcomes in this Canyon View case are invented for instruction. This is not Jordan High data, an assessed submission, or evidence that fieldwork occurred. Illustrative video moments are not recordings or scripts to stage.</p><p>Canyon View High is an invented setting. The candidate, a non-evaluative instructional coach, facilitates four partners as they examine reasoning in Grade 9 writing. Their matched instructional sample includes 24 students: 12 classified as English learners and 12 not classified as English learners. Those are the comparison groups in this example; neither group represents every learner at the school.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence point</th><th scope=\"col\">English learners</th><th scope=\"col\">Peers not classified as EL</th><th scope=\"col\">Total meeting criterion</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence point\">Baseline</td><td data-label=\"English learners\">4/12</td><td data-label=\"Peers not classified as EL\">8/12</td><td data-label=\"Total meeting criterion\">12/24</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence point\">Week 2</td><td data-label=\"English learners\">6/12</td><td data-label=\"Peers not classified as EL\">9/12</td><td data-label=\"Total meeting criterion\">15/24</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence point\">Week 4</td><td data-label=\"English learners\">8/12</td><td data-label=\"Peers not classified as EL\">10/12</td><td data-label=\"Total meeting criterion\">18/24</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence point\">Week 6</td><td data-label=\"English learners\">9/12</td><td data-label=\"Peers not classified as EL\">10/12</td><td data-label=\"Total meeting criterion\">19/24</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The criterion is whether a paragraph explains why its evidence supports the claim. The same sampled learners, comparable writing tasks, common criterion, and recorded scoring discussions support interpretation. This is a descriptive teaching example, not a causal study, a standardized assessment, or a Jordan High report. The later numbers appear here so you can follow the whole case; participants would not know them at the planning stage.</p></section>"}]};
